const e={Spanish:{"Your First Coffee Order":"You're at a small local café. Coffee is often short and strong — a cortado, café solo, or café con leche at the counter.","Arriving at the Airport":'Airport signs are usually in Spanish and English. Information desks can point you to taxis, buses, and currency exchange, so start with "Disculpe, ¿dónde está...?"',"Buying Something at a Corner Store":"This is a small tienda or bodega. The owner is behind the counter — you ask for things rather than browsing.","At the Doctor":'Bring ID and any previous records or prescriptions. Doctors are usually addressed as "doctor" or "doctora", with "usted".',"Ordering at a Restaurant":`Meals are unhurried, and in Spain and much of Latin America the waiter usually won't bring the bill until you ask: "La cuenta, por favor."`,"Asking for Directions":'Locals give directions with landmarks ("next to the church") more than street names.',"Negotiating a Price":'Bargaining is common at open-air and craft markets, but not in shops with marked prices. A polite "¿Me lo deja en...?" (Would you let me have it for...?) is the usual way to ask.',"At the Bank":"Bring ID and any documents the bank asks for. Many branches use a numbered ticket system for queues.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"A little small talk before the interview proper is common. Dress formally and arrive on time.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Expect questions about where you're from and why you're visiting. Asking your host for neighborhood tips is a natural way to start.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Neighbors often chat across fences or in building hallways. Pleasantries about weather and family are common.","Loud Neighbour Confrontation":'Start polite and low-key: a knock and a friendly request ("¿Podrían bajar un poco el volumen, por favor?") before anything stronger.',"Car Accident":"Police and insurance processes vary widely. Exchanging information calmly is the priority.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise the problem quietly and directly with the waiter, and ask for the manager (el encargado / el gerente) if needed.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start with "Disculpe" or "Perdón" before your question.',"Taking the Bus":'Ask the driver or a passenger to confirm your stop: "¿Me avisa cuando lleguemos a...?"',"Calling to Make a Reservation":'Phone calls are still common for reservations. Be ready to spell your name and confirm with "¿Me puede repetir?" if needed.',"Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":'Check the cancellation policy first. Explain the situation politely and ask whether a fee can be waived: "¿Hay alguna posibilidad de...?"',"Getting a Haircut":"Barbershops are traditionally social places. Chatting with the barber about fútbol, family, or the neighborhood is common.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Emergency services exist but response times vary by region. Being clear, calm, and direct when describing location and symptoms is critical.","At the Market":"Markets are lively and social — vendors expect some back-and-forth. Touching produce before buying can be acceptable or not, depending on the vendor.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Dinner is often late: around 9 or 10pm in Spain, and often 8pm or later in much of Latin America. Staying at the table to talk after the meal (sobremesa) is a well-known tradition.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Where taxis have meters, use the meter. Where they don't, agree on the price before you get in. Official taxi stands and ride apps help you avoid surprises.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacists commonly advise on minor ailments and recommend over-the-counter treatment. Which medicines need a prescription varies by country.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":'Many museums offer reduced prices for students, seniors, or residents, and some have free hours. Ask: "¿Hay descuento para estudiantes?"',"Finding an Apartment":"A deposit (fianza in Spain, depósito in much of Latin America) and proof of income or a guarantor (aval/fiador) are commonly required.","Job Interview":"Dress formally and arrive a few minutes early. Show interest in the company as well as your qualifications.","Renting a Car":"Driving customs differ significantly by country. Confirm insurance coverage and ask about local road rules.","At the Pharmacy":"Describe symptoms clearly. Pharmacists often recommend over-the-counter treatment for minor problems and will tell you when you need to see a doctor.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Stay firm but polite, and keep everything in writing: messages, receipts and the contract.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Stay calm, bring your booking confirmation, and ask for the manager if the front desk can't fix it. In Spain, businesses must provide an official complaint form (hoja de reclamaciones) on request.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Ask about the dish's origins and how the cook learned it. Regional recipes often differ, even within one country.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Respectful disagreement is fine. Keep it about the ideas, not the person.","Negotiating a Deal":"Some small talk before getting to the numbers is common. Make your counter-offer clearly and politely.","Debating Current Events":"Current events come up often in social settings. Fútbol is a safe topic almost everywhere; politics can be sensitive, so read the room first.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask where they actually go: "¿Y tú adónde sueles ir?" (where do you usually go?) gets a more personal answer than "¿Qué me recomiendas?"',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":`Explain what you misunderstood and apologize simply. "No lo sabía" (I didn't know) is a useful phrase.`,"Medical Emergency":"Emergency services exist but response times vary. Knowing how to describe the situation clearly and concisely can be critical.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often start with a little small talk before the agenda.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Going out for drinks or tapas after work is a common way to bond with colleagues. Joining for one round is a friendly gesture.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch is a traditional family gathering, often long and multi-course. Bringing wine or dessert and complimenting the cook is customary.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Describe your location clearly, including landmarks and the road name or number.","Doctor's Appointment":"Appointments may run late. Describe symptoms in simple terms. Doctors are addressed formally.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Stay calm and direct, and back your points with reasons.","Live Radio Call-In":"Radio is still popular and call-ins are lively. Being articulate and direct — without rambling — is key.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Government offices often ask for several documents, plus copies. Check what's required, and whether you need an appointment, before you go.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Choose the right time and a private setting. How you say something matters as much as what you say.","Teaching Something":`Informal teaching happens through conversation and demonstration. Starting with "te voy a enseñar" (I'll show you) sets the tone.`,"Throwing a Party":"Parties often start and end late. Guests commonly bring drinks or something to share.","Breaking Up in a Café":"A café is a public place. Keep your voice low and choose a quiet table if you want privacy.","Surprise Birthday Gone Wrong":'If something goes wrong, a bit of humor and a sincere "perdón" help.',"Missed Your Flight":'Stay calm and polite at the airline desk and ask what your options are: "¿Qué opciones tengo?"',"Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bringing a small gift and complimenting the food are common gestures. Use "usted" with the parents until they invite you to use "tú".',"Getting a Job Offer":"Thank them for the offer first, then ask your questions about salary and conditions politely.","Stopped by the Police":"Stay calm, be polite, and have your documents ready. Arguing is never helpful.","Calling in Sick to Work":"In Spain, a doctor's note (justificante médico) or an official sick-leave form (parte de baja) is often required. Call your manager early and keep it brief.","Returning a Broken Item":"Return policies vary widely. Being polite but persistent, and having your receipt, is essential.","Asking Someone Out":`Be clear about what you're inviting them to, e.g. "¿Te gustaría tomar un café conmigo?"`,"Defending Your Thesis":"Prepare clear, structured answers and expect pointed questions from the committee.","Wrong Hotel Room":'Report the issue calmly at reception and say what you need: "Hay un problema con la habitación."',"Catching Someone in a Lie":"Confronting dishonesty directly is acceptable — but how you do it matters. A calm, private conversation works better than a public accusation.","Moving In With a Stranger":"Shared living has its own unwritten rules. Discussing expectations early — cleaning, guests, noise — avoids conflict.","Forgetting an Important Date":"A sincere, personal apology is expected. A small gesture (flowers, a meal) alongside the apology helps.","Lost at the Airport":"Ask airport staff first — they're usually the most reliable help. Having your flight number and destination ready speeds things up.","Being Fired":"Employment rights exist and dismissals follow legal procedures. Staying professional protects your reference and your rights.","Unexpected Proposal":`If you're caught off guard, it's okay to say you need a moment: "Necesito un momento." Kindness matters whatever the answer.`,"Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch is a traditional family gathering and can last for hours. Arrive close to the invited time and compliment the food.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms clearly and precisely, and bring any prior prescriptions. Asking questions about the diagnosis or treatment is fine."},Italian:{"Your First Coffee Order":`Many Italians drink espresso standing at the bar, where it's cheaper than at a table. "Un caffè" means an espresso. Traditionally, cappuccino is a morning drink, not an after-meal one.`,"Ordering at a Restaurant":`Italian menus are divided into courses: antipasto, primo (pasta, risotto), secondo (meat or fish), and dolce. You don't have to order them all. The waiter won't bring the bill until you ask: "Il conto, per favore."`,"Negotiating a Price":`"Può fare un po' di sconto?" works at markets and smaller shops — not in regular stores.`,"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'Greet your host with "Buongiorno" or "Buonasera." Complimenting their city or neighborhood is a nice way to start a conversation.',"At the Doctor":`Italy's national health service (SSN) covers residents; some visits and tests carry a co-pay called a ticket. Doctors are addressed as "Dottore/Dottoressa." Bring your tessera sanitaria (health card) if you have one.`,"Job Interview at a Local Company":'Dress formally and arrive on time. Use "Lei" (formal you) with the interviewer unless invited to use "tu."',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Greeting neighbors with "buongiorno" or "buonasera" in the stairwell is expected. Small talk about the weather or the building is a natural start.',"Loud Neighbour Confrontation":"Start with a polite knock and a calm request. Many apartment buildings set quiet hours in their building rules (regolamento condominiale).","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Dinners are long and social. You don't have to order every course. Coperto (a cover charge per person) on the bill is normal and legal if it's listed on the menu.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Dove andate voi?" (Where do you all go?); the plural shows you want the local answer, not the tourist one.',"Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch is a long family tradition, often several courses. Bring something (wine or pastries from a pasticceria) and expect to stay a while.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often start with some personal conversation, sometimes over espresso. Use titles (Dottore, Ingegnere, Avvocato) until invited not to.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Aperitivo, usually early evening before dinner, is a classic Italian tradition: a spritz or glass of wine, often served with snacks.","Breaking Up in a Café":"A café is a public place. Choose a quiet corner, keep your voice low, and keep it brief and kind.","Throwing a Party":"Hosts traditionally offer plenty of food. Guests often bring wine, dessert, or pastries.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Family ties are traditionally close in Italy. Bring something (wine or pastries), use "Lei" with the parents until told otherwise, and compliment the cooking.',"Arriving at the Airport":'Follow signs for "Uscita" (exit) and "Ritiro bagagli" (baggage claim). Start any question with "Mi scusi." Official taxis are white in most cities.',"Buying Something at a Corner Store":'Greet the owner with "Buongiorno" before asking for anything. The tabaccheria (marked with a "T" sign) also sells bus tickets and stamps.',"Stopping Someone for Help":'Always open with "Mi scusi" before your question; launching straight in is considered rude.',"Taking the Bus":"Validate your ticket immediately -- inspectors do check. Asking fellow passengers for help is completely normal and usually welcomed.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are normal in Italy. Be warm but efficient. Give your name clearly, specify the number in the party, and confirm the time.","At the Bank":"Italian banks are formal and paper-heavy. Bring ID and expect to wait. Address staff formally. Patience is essential.","At the Market":"Greet the vendor first. At many market stalls, the vendor picks the produce for you, so point and ask. In supermarkets, use the disposable gloves provided when handling fruit and vegetables.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Taxis are metered and regulated -- haggling before a metered ride isn't typical. Ask for a receipt (ricevuta). For airport runs, confirm the flat rate upfront.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Italian pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments and over-the-counter treatment.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Major museums often need timed booking. At Italian state museums, under-18s enter free and EU citizens aged 18–25 pay a reduced price. Many state museums are free on the first Sunday of the month.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call rather than message to cancel, apologize briefly, and give as much notice as possible.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise the issue calmly and privately with the waiter. Framing it as a question rather than an accusation works best.","Finding an Apartment":"Personal references and stable income matter. Contracts are formal -- read them carefully. Ask about spese condominiali (building fees).","Job Interview":"Italian professional culture values warmth alongside credentials. Arrive on time, dress well, and make genuine personal connection before business.","Renting a Car":"Confirm what's included in the insurance, check for existing damage, and photograph everything. ZTL zones (restricted traffic areas) will fine you automatically.","Getting a Haircut":"The barbiere or parrucchiere is a social institution. Expect conversation about football, politics, and the neighborhood throughout.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"State discrepancies calmly and specifically. Italian landlords respond better to composed firmness than confrontation. Document everything in writing.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite but specific -- show the paperwork and the discrepancy. Escalate to the direttore if the front desk can't resolve it.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Traditional recipes are a point of pride. For example, classic carbonara uses guanciale, eggs, and pecorino, with no cream. Ask about the regional version of each dish.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Politics, football, and food are all common topics. Respectful disagreement is fine; keep it about the ideas, not the person.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship before talking numbers is common. Be patient, and aim for an agreement where both sides feel respected.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A genuine, personal apology delivered face-to-face lands much better than a formal one. Acknowledge the specific thing that went wrong.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112, the EU emergency number; 118 also reaches medical emergency services. Give your location first, then how many people are hurt and what happened.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch often starts around 1pm and can last well into the afternoon. Bring something from a pasticceria and compliment the food.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle; these are required by law in Italy. Call ACI roadside assistance (803 116) or the number on your rental papers. On the autostrada, give the road number and nearest exit.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms with precision. Bring your tessera sanitaria if you have one. The medico di base (GP) is your first point of contact for most issues.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Hold your position with clear reasons, and stay respectful.","Live Radio Call-In":"Be concise and specific. Make your main point in the first sentence.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Come with every document you think you might need, plus copies. The first clerk may not be the right one. Patience, politeness, and persistence are your only tools.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive conversations are best had face to face, in private. Be honest, stay calm, and listen.","Teaching Something":'Ask questions to check understanding. Showing, not just telling, helps. Saying "ho capito" means "I understood."'},French:{"Your First Coffee Order":`You're at a café terrasse. "Un café" means a small espresso. You stand at the zinc bar for a cheaper price, or sit for table service.`,"Ordering at a Restaurant":"Bread comes with the meal at no charge, and a carafe of tap water (une carafe d'eau) is free on request. Catch the server's eye rather than calling out.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'The French value formality with strangers. Start with "vous" — switch to "tu" only if invited.',"Asking for Directions":'Always begin with "Excusez-moi, monsieur/madame" — jumping straight to the question is considered rude.',"Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Où est-ce que vous iriez, vous?" (Where would YOU go?); the question invites a personal answer rather than the tourist one.',"Job Interview at a Local Company":'French interviews are formal. Dress well, arrive on time, and address the interviewer as "vous" throughout.',"At the Doctor":`France's national health insurance (Assurance Maladie) reimburses a large share of doctor visits. Address the doctor as "Docteur" and use "vous."`,"At the Bank":"French banks are formal. Bring ID and be prepared for paperwork.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise the issue quietly with your server, or ask for the manager (le responsable). A calm, specific complaint works best.","Business Meeting":'French meetings can be formal, with structured debate. Use "vous" and titles until invited otherwise.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":'"On se prend un verre?" (Shall we grab a drink?) is a common invitation. A glass of wine or a draught beer (une pression) is typical.',"Negotiating a Deal":"French negotiations are deliberate and formal. Rushing is seen as disrespectful — patience signals seriousness.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Debating ideas over a meal is a familiar part of French social life. Give reasons for your view and expect them to be questioned.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"French bureaucracy (la paperasse) is notoriously complex. Bring every document you can think of — and expect to come back.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":'In France, the "entrée" is the starter, not the main course (le plat). A typical order is entrée, plat, and then cheese or dessert.',"Breaking Up in a Café":"A quiet café conversation is more appropriate than a very public scene for a difficult personal talk.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bring flowers or wine, dress well, and use "vous" until told otherwise. Table conversation about culture or current events is welcome.',"Arriving at the Airport":"French airports follow strict procedure. Don't try to make small talk with border officers -- efficient and polite is the right register.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Always say 'Bonjour' before anything else -- this is non-negotiable in France. A transaction without it is considered rude. 'Merci, bonne journée' to close.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start every request with "Excusez-moi, monsieur/madame." Skipping the greeting is considered rude in France.',"Taking the Bus":'Validate a paper ticket when you board, or tap your Navigo pass on the reader. "Pardon" and "Excusez-moi" are used constantly.',"Calling to Make a Reservation":'"Est-ce que vous auriez une table pour deux ce soir?" is the right register. If your plans change, call to cancel.',"At the Market":"Always greet the vendor with 'bonjour' and say 'au revoir' when leaving. Comment on the quality of the produce. Vendors respond warmly to genuine interest.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"French taxis are metered and regulated, so haggling isn't normal. In Paris, rides between the airports and the city have official flat fares; check the current rate before you get in.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"French pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments. Describe your symptoms precisely.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":"French apartment culture has clear boundaries. Neighbors exchange polite pleasantries but personal conversation takes longer to develop. 'Bonjour' in the lift is the right level.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"National museums are free for under-18s and for EU residents aged 18–25 with ID. Ask for the tarif réduit. The Louvre requires booking a time slot online.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel as early as you can, by phone or through the booking platform, and apologize briefly. Check whether there's a cancellation fee.","Finding an Apartment":"Expect to provide payslips, tax returns, guarantor documents, and ID. This file is the dossier de location, and an incomplete one is usually passed over.","Job Interview":"French interviews are formal and intellectual. Use 'vous' throughout. Prepare to discuss your parcours as a coherent narrative. Expect to be challenged on your reasoning.","Renting a Car":"Check the état des lieux (vehicle condition report) carefully before signing. Speed cameras are strictly enforced. Fuel is expensive -- ask about the policy for returning it full.","Getting a Haircut":"Describe what you want precisely; bringing a photo helps. Light small talk is normal.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document everything immediately and in writing. French tenants have strong legal protections -- referencing the loi Alur or your droits as a locataire carries weight.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be formal and specific -- reference any written confirmation you have. 'Je souhaite parler au responsable' is your escalation.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"French culinary instruction is precise and demanding. Classic techniques are usually taught a specific, traditional way. Ask questions by showing you've been paying attention.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":`Say what happened, acknowledge it, and move on. "Je ne savais pas" (I didn't know) is a simple, sincere start.`,"Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 15 (SAMU) for medical emergencies, 17 for police, 18 for fire. Describe the situation clearly and calmly. French emergency services expect precise information.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"French Sunday lunch is a multi-hour ritual. Arrive with wine or flowers (not chrysanthemums). Don't rush the meal. The conversation is part of the pleasure.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the high-visibility vest, and place the warning triangle; both are legally required. On autoroutes, use the orange emergency phones or call 112.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"French doctors are thorough and expect you to describe symptoms specifically and logically. The médecin traitant (GP) is your first port of call. Bring your carte vitale if you have one.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Back your position with specific reasons. Calm, precise arguments carry more weight than volume.","Live Radio Call-In":"Get to your point quickly. Make your main argument in the first sentence or two.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive conversations are best had in private. Stay calm and precise, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Explain why as well as how. Expect follow-up questions.","Throwing a Party":"For dinner parties, guests often arrive about 15 minutes after the stated time (le quart d'heure de politesse). Bringing wine, flowers, or dessert is customary."},Portuguese:{"Your First Coffee Order":'In Portugal, "um café" is a short espresso (bica in Lisbon, cimbalino in Porto). Coffee is cheap, serious, and consumed quickly standing at the counter.',"Ordering at a Restaurant":"Portuguese meals are generous. The couvert (bread, olives, butter) appears automatically — it's not free, but you can decline it.","At the Bank":"Portuguese banks are formal. Queues can be long. Having all your documents ready and being patient is essential.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'Greet your host with "Bom dia" or "Boa tarde." Asking about the neighborhood is a good way to start a conversation.',"Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Portions are often generous, and many restaurants offer a meia dose (half portion). The house wine (vinho da casa) is usually affordable.","Business Meeting":'Business settings in Portugal often use formal address ("o senhor" / "a senhora", or a title such as "Doutor" / "Engenheiro"). Arrive on time and prepared.',"Negotiating a Deal":"Negotiation in Portugal tends to be understated — no aggressive back-and-forth. Patience and consistency are more effective.","At the Doctor":"Portugal has a national health service (SNS), and private clinics are also widely used. Describe your symptoms clearly and factually.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring every document plus copies. You'll need a tax number (NIF) for most things. Key offices include the Finanças (tax office) and the Junta de Freguesia (parish council).","Arriving at the Airport":"Greet officials politely with 'bom dia/boa tarde'. Queues are respected. Use 'com licença' first and you'll be helped willingly.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Greet the shopkeeper with 'bom dia' (or 'boa tarde' after lunch) before anything else, and say 'obrigado' / 'obrigada' on the way out.","Stopping Someone for Help":"Start with 'Com licença' or 'Desculpe'. In hilly cities like Lisbon and Porto, directions often use 'lá em cima' (up there) and 'lá em baixo' (down there), and may include stairs.","Taking the Bus":`In Lisbon, the Viva Viagem or Navegante card works on buses, metro, and trams; in Porto, it's the Andante card. Tap or validate when you board. Ask "Este autocarro para em...?" (Does this bus stop at...?)`,"Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are standard. Give your name, party size, and preferred time. Confirm the date explicitly -- 'para sexta-feira, dia 12' avoids ambiguity.","At the Market":"Greet vendors and take your time. Asking 'o que está bom hoje?' (what's good today?) gets you the best quality from the best vendors.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":`Taxis in Portugal are metered and regulated, so haggling isn't normal. Small extra charges (for example, luggage) can be added legally. "Pode dar-me um recibo?" (Can I have a receipt?) is routine.`,"Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":'Portuguese pharmacists can advise on minor ailments and recommend over-the-counter treatment. "Tenho dores de..." (I have pain in...) followed by a precise description works well.',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":"A greeting in the hallway or on the stairs ('bom dia', 'boa tarde') is the usual start. Small talk about the weather or the building is common.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":'Many museums offer discounts for students, seniors, and families, and some have free-entry days. Ask "Tem desconto para...?" (Do you have a discount for...?)',"Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Always cancel in advance and apologize. Portuguese service culture values courtesy -- a brief explanation goes a long way.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues quietly and privately with the waiter. Making a scene is very much frowned upon. A calm, specific complaint is almost always resolved.","Finding an Apartment":"The rental market in Lisbon and Porto is competitive. Bring complete documents: ID, proof of income, and your NIF (tax number).","Job Interview":"Portuguese professional culture is formal and hierarchical. Address interviewers as 'senhor/senhora' plus surname until invited otherwise. Show genuine interest in the company's history.","Renting a Car":"Check the car carefully and photograph any damage. At the pump, gasóleo means diesel and gasolina means gasoline; confirm your car's fuel type first. Many motorways use electronic tolls.","Getting a Haircut":"Portuguese barbershops are social and unhurried. The barber will have opinions about your hair -- listen and engage. Small talk covers football, local news, and weather.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Show interest in the company and in staying to grow there. Be warm and professional, and use formal address until invited otherwise.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document every discrepancy immediately, in writing. Portugal's urban rental law (NRAU) sets out tenant and landlord rights.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite and specific. Show your reservation confirmation. Portuguese hotel staff are professional and will generally resolve genuine billing errors.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Portuguese cuisine has strong regional pride. Ask about the regional origins of dishes -- the cook from Alentejo has different recipes from one in Minho. The history matters.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Listen fully before responding. Respectful disagreement is fine; keep it about the ideas, not the person.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":"Ask 'Onde é que tu costumas ir?' (where do you usually go?) to get a personal recommendation rather than the tourist answer.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A sincere, personal apology is more effective than a formal one. Acknowledge what happened specifically and don't over-explain.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112 for all emergencies in Portugal. Describe your location clearly -- use GPS coordinates if possible as rural road naming can be inconsistent.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"A small draught beer is a fino in Porto and an imperial in Lisbon. A glass of vinho verde is also typical. Leaving after one is fine.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch is a traditional family gathering and can last for hours. Bring wine or a dessert, and try what you're offered.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle. Call the assistance number on your rental or insurance papers, or 112 in an emergency. On motorways, give the road (for example, A1) and the nearest kilometre marker.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms specifically and bring any previous prescriptions or test results. Waits in the national health service (SNS) can be long for non-urgent care, and many people also use private clinics.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your position with clear reasons and stay respectful. Listening fully before you reply goes a long way.","Live Radio Call-In":"Get to your point quickly and be specific. Make your main point in the first sentence.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive topics are often easier face to face, in private, and sometimes over a meal. Choose the moment carefully.","Teaching Something":"Check understanding as you go and invite questions. Being honest about what you don't know builds trust.","Throwing a Party":"Gatherings are often long and generous with food. Guests commonly bring wine or a dessert."},Brazilian:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Café com leite is common at breakfast, and the cafezinho, a small cup of strong, usually sweetened filtered coffee, is drunk throughout the day. Offering a cafezinho to visitors is a common gesture of hospitality.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"Two common formats: rodízio (servers bring rotating dishes, typical at churrascarias) and por quilo (you serve yourself and pay by weight). A 10% service charge is often added to the bill; it's optional.","Negotiating a Price":'In feiras (street markets) and informal shops, "tem desconto?" (any discount?) is perfectly normal and expected.',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Greet neighbors with "Bom dia" or "Oi, tudo bem?" Friendly small talk in the hallway or at the gate is common.',"Job Interview at a Local Company":"Brazilian work culture is relationship-driven. Personal warmth, a firm handshake, and genuine interest in the people you're meeting matter.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"After-work drinks (happy hour) at a boteco, a casual neighborhood bar, are a common way to bond with colleagues. Ice-cold beer shared from big bottles is a classic.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"A weekend churrasco (barbecue) with family and friends is a popular tradition, often lasting all afternoon. Bring dessert or drinks.","Business Meeting":"Brazilian meetings often start with personal conversation. Jumping to the agenda immediately can feel abrupt.","Throwing a Party":"Parties usually feature plenty of food and cold drinks. Guests often bring something to share, and the start time is usually flexible.","Medical Emergency":"Call 192 for SAMU (ambulance). The public health system (SUS) is free; UPAs (Unidades de Pronto Atendimento) handle urgent but non-critical cases. Waits can be long.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Arroz, feijão (rice and beans), and farofa are everyday staples. A couvert (bread, appetizers) may arrive automatically; if you don't want it, send it back so it isn't charged.","Arriving at the Airport":"Large airports like São Paulo–Guarulhos (GRU) have official taxi desks and ride-app pickup points (Uber, 99). Use those rather than accepting rides from people approaching you.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":'The padaria (bakery) is a neighborhood staple for coffee, pão de queijo, and snacks. Greet with "Bom dia" or "Boa tarde" before ordering.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'Greet your host warmly; "Oi, tudo bem?" is the standard friendly opener. Asking about the neighborhood is a good way to start a conversation.',"At the Doctor":"Many people use private health insurance (plano de saúde) alongside the public system (SUS). Describe your symptoms clearly and in order.","Stopping Someone for Help":"Start with 'Com licença' or 'Desculpa' before your question. Directions often use landmarks (a padaria, a posto de gasolina) as well as street names.","Taking the Bus":'São Paulo uses the Bilhete Único card and Rio uses Riocard. On buses, pull the cord or press the button to request your stop. Ask "Esse ônibus passa por...?" (Does this bus go by...?)',"Calling to Make a Reservation":"A friendly greeting before your request is normal. Give your name, party size, and time, and confirm the date.","At the Bank":"Bring your CPF (tax ID) and ID. Pix, Brazil's instant payment system, is used everywhere, and most banking is done through apps.","At the Market":"The feira (street market) is a weekly social event. Greet vendors warmly. Asking about what's freshest gets you the best quality from the best vendors.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Ride apps (Uber, 99) show the price upfront. City taxis use meters. For long trips, agree on the fare before you leave.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":'Pharmacists can advise on minor ailments. Ask for the genérico (generic) version for a lower price; generic packaging carries a large "G."',"Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Many museums are free or inexpensive, and some have free days; MASP in São Paulo, for example, is known for free Tuesdays. Check current hours before you go.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel as early as you can with a short, friendly explanation. A call or message both work at most places.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues politely and privately with the waiter, and ask for the gerente (manager) if needed.","Finding an Apartment":"Landlords usually require a guarantor (fiador), rental insurance (seguro-fiança), or a deposit (caução). By law, the deposit can't exceed three months' rent.","Job Interview":"A little small talk before the interview proper is common. Dress formally and use 'o senhor' / 'a senhora' until invited otherwise.","Renting a Car":"Check the insurance and photograph any damage. In São Paulo, cars are banned from central streets at peak hours on one weekday based on the last digit of the license plate (rodízio).","Getting a Haircut":"Brazilian barbershops (barbearias) are social and skilled. The barber will have opinions and engage you in conversation. Tipping is appreciated.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship before talking numbers is common. Be patient and friendly.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":"Ask 'Aonde você costuma ir?' (where do you usually go?) to get past the tourist answer. Asking what's typical where they're from is a natural follow-up.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A direct, personal apology works best: 'Desculpa, não foi minha intenção' (sorry, I didn't mean it). Explain what you misunderstood and ask how it's usually done.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 192 (SAMU, ambulance), 193 (fire/rescue), or 190 (police). Describe your location precisely and stay on the line.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Call your insurance breakdown assistance line. On federal highways (BRs), the highway police can be called on 191. Stay visible and keep hazard lights on.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Private clínicas are efficient for non-emergency care. Describe symptoms specifically. Bring any prior documentation and your CPF.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your position with specific reasons and stay respectful. Football and politics are the topics most likely to get heated.","Live Radio Call-In":"Be direct and specific. Make your main point in the first sentence.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring complete documents and copies. Notary offices (cartórios) handle many official records and signature certifications, and many people hire a despachante (paperwork agent) to help.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Choose the right time and a private setting. Speak honestly and kindly, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Use everyday examples, check understanding with 'Entendeu?' or 'Faz sentido?', and ask what the other person already knows.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday family lunch is a common tradition. Bring something (dessert or drinks), come hungry, and compliment the food.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document discrepancies in writing immediately. Rentals are governed by the Tenancy Law (Lei do Inquilinato, Lei 8.245/1991). A lawyer (advogado) or the consumer protection agency (Procon) can advise you.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite and specific. Show any written confirmation you have. Brazilian hotel management responds well to calm, warm, documented complaints.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Brazilian cuisine varies enormously by state -- cozinha mineira is completely different from bahiana. Ask where the chef is from. The regional food story is the point.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Football, politics, and social issues come up often. Respectful disagreement is fine; knowing the specifics earns respect."},Creole:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Haitian coffee is strong and often very sweet. Street vendors and small shops serve it in small cups throughout the day.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'Greetings matter: say "Bonjou" (before midday) or "Bonswa" (afternoon and evening), and ask how the family is. Accepting a drink when offered is polite.',"Asking for Directions":"In Haiti, directions use neighborhood names and landmarks more than street addresses.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Greet first ('Bonjou' / 'Bonswa') before anything else. Asking about family ('Kouman fanmi an ye?') is a common part of neighborly conversation.","At the Doctor":"Healthcare access in Haiti can be limited. Doctors and nurses are highly respected. Be patient and bring any existing medical records.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Government processes in Haiti can require patience and multiple trips. Having copies of all documents and staying calm is essential.","Throwing a Party":"Celebrations usually include music, dancing, and plenty of food. Konpa is the classic party music.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Staples include griyo (fried pork), diri kole ak pwa (rice and beans), bannann peze (fried plantain), and pikliz (spicy pickled slaw).","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":"Respect for elders is fundamental in Haitian culture. Address parents formally, bring a gift, and show interest in their family.","Medical Emergency":"Emergency care resources are limited. Knowing the nearest clinic and having basic first aid knowledge is genuinely important in Haiti.","Arriving at the Airport":`At Toussaint Louverture International Airport in Port-au-Prince, many people may offer to carry bags or find you a ride. "Mèsi, m pa bezwen" (thanks, I don't need it) politely declines.`,"Buying Something at a Corner Store":"The ti boutik (small corner store) is the social and commercial hub of every neighborhood. Greet the owner warmly before asking for anything.","Stopping Someone for Help":"Start with 'Bonjou' and 'Eskize m' (excuse me) before asking for help. Directions often use landmarks as well as street names.","Taking the Bus":`Tap-taps, colorfully painted shared trucks and buses, are the main public transport. Ask where it's going before you board. Saying "Mèsi chofè!" tells the driver you want to get off.`,"Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are common at better restaurants. Be warm and clear -- give your name, party size, and time. Confirming the day before is good practice.","At the Bank":'Queues can be long, so arrive early. Always bring your national ID or passport, and greet staff with "Bonjou."',"At the Market":"The mache (market) is lively, and bargaining is normal. Counter a price politely and with a smile rather than dismissing it.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Negotiate the fare before you get in -- there are no meters in most Haitian taxis. State your destination and ask 'Konbyen?' (how much?). A warm counter-offer is expected.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacies are often the first stop for minor health problems, and pharmacists give direct advice. Describe your symptoms precisely.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Cultural institutions include MUPANAH in Port-au-Prince. Entry is affordable. Ask about guided tours -- local guides provide context that signs don't.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancellation culture in Haiti is informal -- a phone call with a warm explanation and apology is all that's expected.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues quietly and with warmth -- Haitian hospitality culture means staff genuinely want to fix problems. Public confrontation is very much frowned upon.","Finding an Apartment":"Many rentals are found through personal connections. Inspect everything carefully and get the terms in writing before you pay.","Job Interview":"Interviews are usually formal. Address interviewers as 'Msye' or 'Madan' plus surname (French 'Monsieur' / 'Madame' is also common in professional settings). Some greeting and small talk before business is normal.","Renting a Car":"Renting a car is common for business or travel between cities. Roads vary enormously in quality. Always check the vehicle thoroughly before accepting it.","Getting a Haircut":"Barbershops are social places where neighborhood news, football, and politics are discussed. Chatting with your barber is normal.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Personal connections can matter. If you have a mutual contact, mention it naturally. Show respect and genuine interest in the company.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"If the apartment doesn't match the description, raise it directly but politely with the landlord. Keep receipts and put agreements in writing.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite and specific. Show any written confirmation you have. Haitian business culture responds well to calm, respectful persistence.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Soup joumou, eaten on January 1, celebrates Haiti's independence in 1804 and is on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list. Ask the cook about the history behind each dish.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"History, especially the Haitian Revolution and independence in 1804, is a central part of Haitian national history. Listening as much as speaking shows respect.","Negotiating a Deal":"Relationships matter. Friendly humor helps, and a polite counter-offer is normal in informal deals.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":"Ask 'Kote ou menm ou konn ale?' (where do you usually go?) to get a personal recommendation rather than the answer for visitors.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"Dignity and respect matter. A sincere, face-to-face apology that names what went wrong is the best way to repair things.","Helping at an Accident Scene":`Emergency services are limited, especially outside Port-au-Prince. Call for help clearly ("Ede m! Gen aksidan!" Help me! There's been an accident!) and give the exact location.`,"Business Meeting":"Meetings usually start with greetings and personal conversation. Use titles and surnames (Msye, Madan, or French Monsieur, Madame) until invited to drop them.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Prestige is the iconic Haitian beer, and Barbancourt is the famous Haitian rum. Football is a safe topic.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday is traditionally a family day. Lunch often features rice and beans, griyo, and pikliz. Bringing something is respectful.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Roadside assistance infrastructure is limited. Your best resource is your network -- calling someone who knows the area. On main roads, other drivers will often stop to help.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms specifically: where it hurts, since when, and how bad. Bring any previous medical records. Many people use private clinics for non-emergency care.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your position with specific reasons and stay respectful. Knowing the facts carries weight.","Live Radio Call-In":"Radio is a major source of news in Haiti, and call-in shows are popular. Be direct and specific.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Address older relatives with respect (titles like 'Tonton' and 'Matant' are common). Choosing the right time and place matters as much as the words.","Teaching Something":"Respect what the learner already knows, and connect new ideas to practical, everyday examples."},Parisian:{"Your First Coffee Order":'Drinking at the counter (au comptoir) is cheaper than sitting at a table or on the terrasse. "Un café" is an espresso. A café crème is usually a morning order.',"Ordering at a Restaurant":"A typical meal is entrée (starter), plat (main), and dessert. The fixed-price menu (la formule or le menu) is usually the best value. Substitutions are less common than in North America; ask politely if you have an allergy.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":'In Paris apartment buildings, a polite "bonjour" in the elevator or stairwell is the norm. Longer chats happen more in village-like neighborhoods and local shops.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":'"On se prend un verre?" (Shall we grab a drink?) is the classic invitation. A glass of wine or a draught beer at a nearby café is typical.',"Negotiating a Deal":"In Paris, price negotiation is acceptable at marchés and antique dealers — not in shops. Being charming and unhurried goes further than being firm.","Asking for Directions":`Always begin with "Excusez-moi, madame/monsieur." If the answer is too fast, ask: "Vous pouvez parler plus lentement, s'il vous plaît?"`,"Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Vous avez un endroit que vous aimez vraiment?" (Do you have a place you really like?) to get a personal answer rather than the guidebook one.',"At the Market":"Parisian marchés (Marché d'Aligre, Marché des Enfants Rouges) are social rituals. Greet the vendor before asking for anything. Commenting on the quality of produce is welcomed and expected.","Business Meeting":"French meetings can be long and debate-heavy. Disagreement is seen as intellectual engagement, not hostility. Hierarchy is respected — address senior people formally throughout. Arrive on time.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":`Parisian dinners follow a structure: entrée, plat, then cheese or dessert. Taking time between courses is expected. The waiter will not rush you. Asking for the bill ("l'addition, s'il vous plaît") is how you end.`,"Job Interview at a Local Company":`French interviews are formal and intellectually rigorous. Dress impeccably. Expect pointed questions about your motivation. Use "vous" throughout. Showing you know the company's history impresses.`,"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":`Parisians start formal — "vous" until invited to use "tu". Once comfortable they're warm and curious. A genuine question about their neighborhood opens everything up.`,"Dealing with Bureaucracy":"French bureaucracy (la paperasse) is notoriously complex. Bring every document imaginable. The préfecture, mairie, and CAF each have their own systems. Politeness is essential — impatience makes things worse.","Restaurant Complaint":`Raise the issue quietly with your server, or ask for the manager (le responsable). "Je suis un peu déçu" (I'm a little disappointed) sets the right tone.`,"Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bring good wine or quality chocolates — not supermarket flowers. Dress well. Use "vous" until explicitly invited otherwise. Engage in table conversation about culture, food, or current events.',"Breaking Up in a Café":"Choose a quiet corner and keep your voice low. Calm and direct is best in a public place.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch can last a few hours. Bring wine (ask what they're cooking first) and compliment the food.","Arriving at the Airport":"CDG is large and can be disorientating. The RER B takes you to central Paris efficiently. Use the official taxi rank. Address officials and staff briefly and formally.","At the Bank":"Parisian banks are formal and process-heavy. Bring your passport and all relevant documents. Address staff as 'monsieur' or 'madame'. Expect to wait.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Paris taxis are metered and regulated, so haggling isn't normal. Rides between the airports (CDG, Orly) and Paris have official flat fares; check the current rate before you get in.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments. Describe your symptoms precisely.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The Louvre requires booking a time slot online, and booking ahead is recommended at the Musée d'Orsay. National museums are free for under-18s and for EU residents aged 18–25.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel by phone, with a brief apology and explanation. Parisian restaurants take reservations seriously -- a last-minute no-show is genuinely rude.","Finding an Apartment":"The Paris rental market is among Europe's most competitive. A complete dossier is non-negotiable -- three payslips, tax returns, ID, and often a guarantor.","Job Interview":"Parisian interviews are formal, intellectual, and demanding. Use 'vous' throughout. Present your parcours as a coherent intellectual narrative. Expect to be challenged on your reasoning.","Renting a Car":"Paris has a low-emission zone: cars need a Crit'Air sticker. Speed cameras are common on French roads. Keep your license, insurance, and rental papers in the car.","Getting a Haircut":"Describe what you want precisely; bringing a photo helps. Light small talk is normal.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document discrepancies immediately and in writing. Reference the loi Alur and your droits as a locataire. The ADIL offers free legal advice.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be formal and precise. Show written confirmation. 'Je souhaite parler avec le directeur' is your escalation.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Parisian culinary instruction is technically precise and serious about method. Ask about the history and regional origins of techniques -- it will be appreciated.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Open disagreement is a normal part of discussion. Defend a clear position while acknowledging counterpoints. Café discussions about politics and culture are a long-standing Paris tradition.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A direct, calm explanation usually works best. State clearly what happened, apologize simply, and move on.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 15 (SAMU) for medical emergencies, or 112. State your location precisely: arrondissement, street, and nearest cross street.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday family lunch is a long, multi-course tradition. Arrive with good wine, and expect conversation to continue long after the meal.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Call your insurance breakdown assistance or use the autoroute emergency phones. Hazard lights and a warning triangle are legally required.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"The médecin traitant (registered GP) is your first contact. Describe symptoms logically and specifically. Bring your carte vitale if you have it.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Defend your position with specific reasoning and acknowledge good counterpoints. Calm and precise carries more weight than volume.","Live Radio Call-In":"France Inter, Europe 1, and RTL are among the major national stations. Get to your point quickly and be specific.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Choose the right moment and a private setting. Stay calm and precise, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Explaining the underlying principle before the technique works well. Expect follow-up questions.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Always say 'Bonjour' before anything else -- this is non-negotiable in Paris. A transaction without it is considered rude. 'Merci, bonne journée' to close.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start every request with "Excusez-moi, monsieur/madame." Skipping the greeting is considered rude.',"Taking the Bus":"Validate your ticket immediately. RATP buses require a validated Navigo card or single ticket. 'Pardon' and 'Excusez-moi' are used constantly.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Reservations are standard at sit-down restaurants, and many places book online. If your plans change, cancel; a no-show is considered rude."},"Southern French":{"Your First Coffee Order":"Café terraces are a big part of daily life in the South. Pastis, an anise-flavored apéritif, is the region's classic drink, usually served before lunch or dinner.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Greet neighbors with a "bonjour" and a smile. In small towns, chatting at the market or the boulangerie is part of daily life.',"Throwing a Party":"Southern French gatherings revolve around food — especially rosé wine, tapenade, grilled fish, and seasonal vegetables. Long outdoor lunches are a tradition in the warmer months.","Ordering at a Restaurant":`Southern cooking leans on Mediterranean ingredients: olive oil, tomatoes, herbes de Provence, and fresh fish. Asking "Qu'est-ce qui est frais aujourd'hui?" (What's fresh today?) is a good start.`,"At the Market":"The weekly marché is a fixture of southern towns: produce, cheese, olives, lavender, and savon de Marseille. Arrive early for the best selection.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch can last the whole afternoon: apéritif (often pastis), several courses, then cheese and dessert. Bring wine or a tarte.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"In summer, dining outside on a terrace is common, and dinner often starts later than in winter. A glass of rosé from Provence is a regional classic.","Business Meeting":'Some small talk before business is normal. Use "vous" and formal titles until invited otherwise.',"At the Doctor":"French public healthcare extends throughout the South. Doctors are thorough and formal. Bring your carte vitale. Private specialists exist for faster appointments.","Asking for Directions":'Always start with "Bonjour" before your question. Directions often use landmarks like the church, the square, or the mairie (town hall).',"Negotiating a Price":'At antique markets and brocantes, negotiation is part of the ritual. A calm "vous pouvez faire un geste?" (can you make a gesture?) often gets a small discount.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Ask your host about the local market day; most towns have a weekly one, and it's a great way to explore.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"The apéro, an early-evening drink with olives or snacks, is a well-loved tradition. Pastis and rosé are classic choices.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship before getting to the point is common. Sharing a meal can help, but get the final terms in writing.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask where they actually eat, not the tourist spots: "Vous allez où, vous ?" Local products like olive oil, wine and tapenade are good topics.',"Job Interview at a Local Company":"A bit of small talk before getting to business is normal. Showing genuine interest in the region and its quality of life can work in your favour.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Arrive early, bring every document plus copies, and be patient. A polite, friendly tone helps.","Restaurant Complaint":"In the South, making a complaint at a restaurant requires tact. Lead with something positive about the meal, then raise the issue calmly. Raised voices and confrontation will embarrass everyone at the table. A quiet word with the patron is the local way.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bring wine, flowers, or dessert. Compliment the food and ask about regional dishes. Use "vous" until invited otherwise.',"Breaking Up in a Café":"Café tables are close together, so pick a quiet spot and keep your voice low.","Arriving at the Airport":'Nice, Marseille, and Montpellier are the main airports in the South. A simple "Bonjour" before any question is expected.',"At the Bank":'Banking in the South of France follows standard French procedure. Formal address is still expected — use "vous" throughout. Appointments are recommended for anything beyond basic transactions.',"Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Taxis are metered and regulated. For long trips to villages or airports, ask for a price estimate before you leave.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"French pharmacists are trained to advise on minor ailments and recommend treatment. Describe your symptoms clearly.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The South is rich in Roman sites, like the Arena of Nîmes and the Pont du Gard. Many sites offer reduced prices for students and young EU residents; ask for the tarif réduit.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel as early as you can. Calling small restaurants is often better than messaging. Apologize briefly.","Finding an Apartment":"Rentals in Marseille, Montpellier, and Nice are competitive, especially near the coast. Expect to provide a full dossier: ID, payslips, and often a guarantor.","Job Interview":'Show interest in the company and the region. Use "vous," arrive on time, and be ready to discuss your experience in detail.',"Renting a Car":"Renting a car is almost essential for exploring the southern countryside, villages, and coast. Agencies at airports are standard. Be clear about insurance options — the staff will explain them but sometimes quickly. Ask them to slow down if needed.","Getting a Haircut":"Describe what you want clearly. Light conversation about the neighborhood, football, or the weather is normal.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"If a listing doesn't match what you were shown, raise it calmly and in writing. French tenant law (including the loi ALUR) protects renters, and the local ADIL gives free housing advice.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Raise billing issues calmly and privately, with your booking confirmation in hand. Ask for the manager if needed.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Southern dishes are tied to season and place: ratatouille from Nice, bouillabaisse from Marseille, tapenade, and daube. Ask about the origin of each ingredient.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Football, politics, and food are all common topics. Respectful disagreement is fine; keep it about the ideas.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"Say what happened, acknowledge it, and move on. A simple, honest explanation is usually well received.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"French law requires you to help someone in danger if you can do so safely. Call 15 (SAMU), 18 (fire), or 112. Describe the location clearly and follow the dispatcher's instructions.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch often starts around 1pm and can last well into the afternoon, with several courses and regional wines. Try a bit of everything.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle. Call the assistance number on your rental or insurance papers. On autoroutes, use the orange emergency phones or call 112.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":'French medical consultations are thorough and formal. Use "vous" throughout. Describe symptoms clearly and chronologically. Having your symptoms and their timeline ready before you arrive helps.',"Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your ground with clear reasons, and humor helps. Finding common ground is often the goal.","Live Radio Call-In":"Local radio covers regional news and football, especially Olympique de Marseille. Get to your point quickly.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Be direct but kind, choose a private moment, and let the other person finish before you respond.","Teaching Something":"Connect your explanation to local examples like food, places, and customs. Patience and humor help.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Corner shops in southern towns are often family-run and deeply local. A proper bonjour when entering and au revoir when leaving is non-negotiable. Regular customers are greeted by name — being warm and consistent builds real rapport.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Open with "Excusez-moi" and a smile. Directions often use landmarks rather than street names.',"Taking the Bus":"Marseille, Montpellier, and Nice all have city bus and tram networks. Validate your ticket when you board.","Calling to Make a Reservation":'Start with "Bonjour," then give the number of guests, the date and time, and your name.'},"Quebec French":{"Your First Coffee Order":`"Un café" here usually means a regular drip coffee, not espresso. Tim Hortons is culturally iconic, and Montreal's Mile-End and Plateau neighborhoods have many independent cafés. In winter, coffee is consumed partly just to warm up.`,"Ordering at a Restaurant":"Quebec restaurants range from casual cabanes à sucre (sugar shacks) to world-class Montreal gastronomy. Portions are generous. Poutine, tourtière (meat pie), and maple syrup feature prominently — these are genuine cultural staples, not just tourist food. Table d'hôte (prix fixe) menus are common and excellent value.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":`Small talk about the weather, especially winter — "c'est encore une bordée!" — is a common conversation starter. Asking "ça va?" with genuine curiosity opens conversation naturally.`,"Asking for Directions":'Start with "Excusez-moi." In cities, metro stations and neighborhood names are common reference points. Expect Quebec French, which sounds different from the French of France.',"At the Market":"The Jean-Talon market in Montreal and local farmers markets across Quebec are community hubs. Local produce, maple products, and artisan cheeses (including Oka and various fromageries) are points of genuine pride. Vendors are friendly and happy to explain their products.","Negotiating a Price":"Fixed pricing is standard in most Quebec commerce. At marchés aux puces (flea markets) and antique fairs, light negotiation is acceptable but not expected the way it is in market cultures elsewhere.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":'"Prendre un verre" after work is social currency in Quebec. Draft beer (une pinte) or a local Quebec craft beer is the standard. Conversation becomes warm and personal quickly. The dépanneur (corner store) sells beer and is its own Quebec institution.',"Throwing a Party":"Gatherings usually feature plenty of food and local beer. In spring, a party at a cabane à sucre (sugar shack) during maple season is a classic Quebec tradition.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday gatherings in Quebec are relaxed and family-centered. Tourtière, pea soup, or a slow-cooked meal. Maple syrup appears somewhere. Conversations about hockey, Quebec politics, and neighborhood news are all expected and welcome.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Montreal is known for its restaurants. Table d'hôte (set) menus are very common. Quebec cuisine features game meats, maple, local cheeses, and Québécois comfort food alongside strong international influences.","Business Meeting":"Meetings start relatively on time but open with genuine small talk. French is the working language — switching to English without invitation can be noted.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Quebec professional culture expects French as the primary language (Bill 101). Dress professionally but not stiffly. Showing genuine interest in Quebec's distinctness from the rest of Canada goes over well.","At the Doctor":"Quebec public healthcare (RAMQ) covers most residents. Family doctor access is strained — many use clinics sans rendez-vous (walk-in clinics) for non-emergency care. Describe symptoms clearly in French.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Quebec government services (SAAQ, Revenu Québec, Services Québec) operate mainly in French. Have all your documents ready; many services are available online.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Quebec restaurant reservations are standard and worth making at any sit-down place. Be warm but efficient — give name, party size, and time. Most restaurants have online booking now.","Getting a Haircut":"Hockey is a safe topic almost anywhere, especially the Montreal Canadiens.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":"Ask where they actually go, not what's famous. Montreal neighborhoods (Plateau, Mile-End, Rosemont, Outremont) each have their own character.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":"Come with wine or a quality dessert. Expect warmth and directness — formal titles drop quickly. Complimenting the food genuinely and asking about the family's history in Quebec will be warmly received.","Breaking Up in a Café":"A café is a public place. Choose a quiet table and keep your voice low if you want privacy.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Quebec politics, the French-English dynamic, hockey, and environmental issues are all live conversation topics. Knowing the difference between provincial and federal politics shows real engagement.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Traditional Quebec dishes include tourtière (meat pie), ragoût de pattes (pork hock stew), and cipaille (layered meat pie). Ask about maple syrup grades: golden, amber, dark, and very dark.","Medical Emergency":"Call 911 for emergencies. For non-urgent health questions, call Info-Santé at 811 to speak with a nurse. RAMQ covers residents; visitors need travel insurance. Emergency rooms (urgences) can have long waits.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start with "Excusez-moi" and then your question. In Montreal, directions often reference metro stations and neighborhood names.',"Taking the Bus":"Montreal's STM runs the metro and buses, and the OPUS card is the standard fare card. Downtown, the underground pedestrian network (RÉSO) links metro stations, malls, and buildings, which helps in winter.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":'A direct, warm apology works well in Quebec. Acknowledging the specific cultural difference — "je ne savais pas que..." — is usually appreciated.',"Difficult Family Conversation":"Choose a private moment and be direct but kind. Listen fully before you respond.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Bring wine or dessert and plan to stay a while. Hockey and local life are safe topics; Quebec sovereignty and language politics can be sensitive, so read the room.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms clearly in French and bring your health insurance card (carte d'assurance maladie). RAMQ covers most residents; walk-in clinics (cliniques sans rendez-vous) handle many non-emergency visits.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Ask your host about their favourite local spot or the nearby market; it's an easy way into a real conversation.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":'Quebec taxis are metered and regulated. Uber operates in Montreal and Quebec City. No price negotiation expected for regular city trips. "Pouvez-vous me donner un reçu?" for a receipt is standard.',"Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Quebec pharmacists are approachable and can advise on symptoms. They can also prescribe treatment for certain minor conditions without a doctor's visit.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues calmly and directly with the server. Being polite but clear about the issue gets results.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":'The dépanneur is a Quebec institution — sells groceries, beer, lottery tickets, and everything in between. Warm but brief interactions. "Bonjour" before anything else is non-negotiable.',"Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your ground with specific reasons and keep it friendly. Quebec sovereignty and language politics are the most sensitive topics."},Neapolitan:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Naples is famous for its espresso, usually drunk quickly at the bar. Caffè sospeso, paying for an extra coffee for a stranger who can't afford one, is a Neapolitan tradition.","Ordering at a Restaurant":`Naples is the birthplace of pizza, and the art of the Neapolitan pizzaiuolo is on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list. The margherita and the marinara are the classics. Street pizza is often folded "a portafoglio" (like a wallet).`,"Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Many older Neapolitan neighborhoods have bassi, ground-floor homes that open straight onto the street, so neighbors often chat right outside their doors.","Asking for Directions":'Start with "Mi scusi." The historic center is a maze of narrow streets, so ask for landmarks like a piazza or church, not just street names.',"At the Market":"Porta Nolana is Naples' famous fish market, and La Pignasecca is one of the city's oldest street markets. Knowing a few food names in Italian helps a lot.","Negotiating a Price":"At street markets, asking for a small discount is normal. In regular shops, prices are fixed.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Neapolitan ragù is traditionally slow-cooked for hours, often for the Sunday family lunch. Come hungry and try what you're offered.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"An aperitivo after work is a common way to socialize. Football, and SSC Napoli above all, is a safe and popular topic.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Famous Neapolitan desserts include sfogliatella (a ridged, filled pastry) and pastiera (a ricotta and wheat tart traditionally made at Easter). The bill comes only when you ask.","Throwing a Party":"Celebrations usually center on food and family. Bringing pastries from a pasticceria is always welcome.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often start with personal conversation and an espresso. Use formal titles until invited otherwise.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Family ties are traditionally close. Bring pastries from a pasticceria, use "Lei" with the parents, and compliment the cooking.',"Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring every document plus copies, and expect more than one visit. Patience and politeness help.","Arriving at the Airport":"Naples International Airport (Capodichino) is close to the city. The Alibus shuttle runs to the center and the port, and official taxis have fixed fares for set routes; confirm before you leave.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Greet the shopkeeper warmly first and be brief. The local alimentari is a social hub in Naples -- the owner often knows the whole building.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":'Greet your host with "Buongiorno" or "Buonasera." Asking for neighborhood tips, like the best pizzeria nearby, is a great conversation starter.',"At the Doctor":"Public healthcare is run locally by the ASL and can have long waits; many people also use private clinics. Describe your symptoms in standard Italian.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Always start with "Scusi." Directions often come with landmarks like piazzas, churches, and metro stations.',"Taking the Bus":'ANM runs the city buses and Metro Line 1, whose "Art Stations" (like Toledo) are attractions themselves. Validate your ticket and allow extra time.',"Calling to Make a Reservation":"Neapolitan restaurants answer phones warmly and directly. State your reservation details clearly. A 'grazie, a presto' closes the call.","At the Bank":"Neapolitan banks can be slow but staff are usually warm. Bring full ID. For most routine transactions, the bank app is faster.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Official taxis have meters; agree on the price for airport runs upfront. 'Quanto mi fai pagare?' (how much will you charge me?) is the right opening for private transport.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The National Archaeological Museum (MANN) holds treasures from Pompeii and Herculaneum. Book online, and note that Italian state museums are free on the first Sunday of each month.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call personally and be warm. Neapolitan hospitality means your cancellation matters -- a brief, genuine explanation is all that's needed.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues politely and privately with the waiter. Framing it as a question works better than an accusation.","Finding an Apartment":"Naples rental market has plenty of informal arrangements. Personal connections matter. Inspect everything carefully including utilities, water pressure, and heating.","Job Interview":"Personal warmth matters alongside credentials. A referral from a mutual contact can help.","Renting a Car":"Much of the historic center is a ZTL (restricted traffic zone) that fines unauthorized cars automatically, and parking is hard. For the Amalfi Coast, buses and ferries are alternatives to driving.","Getting a Haircut":"The local barbiere is a neighborhood fixture. Conversation about football and the neighborhood is normal.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"A referral from a mutual contact can help. Be warm and professional.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document everything immediately and in writing. A local lawyer (avvocato) or tenants' association can advise you on your rights.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be specific and calm. Show your documentation. 'Vorrei parlare con il direttore' is your escalation.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Naples is the home of pizza, ragù, and sfogliatella. Traditional methods are a point of pride, so ask about the origins and history of each dish.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Football (Napoli), politics, and the North–South divide are common topics. Respectful disagreement is fine.","Negotiating a Deal":"Friendliness and humor help. A firm counter-offer, made politely, is normal.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Tu dove vai di solito?" (where do you usually go?) and ask for their personal favorite pizzeria or pasticceria.',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A direct, sincere, face-to-face apology works best. Say what happened and move forward.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112, the EU emergency number; 118 also reaches medical emergency services. Give the location first, then what happened.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch is a long family tradition. Arrive with pastries from a pasticceria and expect several courses.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle. Call ACI roadside assistance (803 116) or the number on your rental or insurance papers.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms specifically -- precision matters. The ASL Naples can have long waits. Bring any prior documentation and your tessera sanitaria.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Hold your position with specific facts and stay respectful.","Live Radio Call-In":"Local radio covers football, politics, and neighborhood events. Specific local knowledge, like neighborhoods and street names, helps you make your point.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive conversations are best held face to face, at home. Be honest and kind, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Expect direct, practical questions. Show examples and stay enthusiastic."},"Dominican Republic":{"Your First Coffee Order":"Dominican coffee is usually brewed in a greca (stovetop coffee pot) and served strong and sweet. Café con leche is common at breakfast.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"The colmado is the neighborhood corner store, often with music and people hanging out. Regulars sometimes buy on credit (fiado). Many colmados also deliver.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Asking "¿Cómo está la familia?" is a classic friendly opener. Chatting outside or from balconies is common.',"Negotiating a Price":"Bargaining is normal at markets and with informal vendors. Keep it friendly. In regular stores, prices are fixed.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Presidente is the iconic Dominican beer, and Brugal and Barceló are well-known Dominican rums. Merengue and bachata are the soundtrack of most nights out.","Throwing a Party":"Merengue and bachata, both on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list, are party staples. Food is central, and start times are flexible.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"La Bandera (rice, beans, and stewed meat) is the classic lunch, and lunch is traditionally the main meal of the day. Ask about the plato del día (dish of the day).","Asking for Directions":'Directions usually rely on landmarks and neighborhood names rather than street addresses. Start with "Disculpe."',"At the Market":"Mercados sell fresh tropical fruit, yuca, plátano and meat, and vendors often call out their prices. Knowing a few food names helps.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sancocho, a hearty stew with several meats and root vegetables, is a classic dish for Sundays and gatherings. Bring something sweet or drinks.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Mangú (mashed plantain) with los tres golpes (fried eggs, fried cheese, and salami) is a classic Dominican dish. A full rice-and-beans plate is also common.","Calling to Make a Reservation":'Phone calls are still standard for reservations. "¿Me puede ayudar?" is a polite opener, and confirming the day before is common.',"Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call to cancel, apologize briefly and explain. Ask whether there is any fee.","Getting a Haircut":"Barbershops (barberías) are social spots. Baseball and neighborhood news are common topics.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":`Ask "¿Y tú, pa' dónde vas los fines de semana?" (where do you go on weekends?) to get a personal recommendation.`,"Job Interview at a Local Company":"Referrals and personal connections are common in hiring. Dress formally, arrive on time, and show interest in the company.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often start with personal conversation about family or baseball before the agenda. Use titles like Licenciado or Ingeniero until invited otherwise.","At the Doctor":"Both public and private healthcare exist; private clinics usually require payment or insurance upfront. Bring any previous records.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Government offices and banks often require several documents and sometimes more than one visit. Going with someone who knows the process helps.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Be respectful to elders, use "usted," and bring a gift. Showing interest in the family is appreciated.',"Arriving at the Airport":'The main airports are Las Américas (Santo Domingo), Punta Cana, and Cibao (Santiago). People may offer to carry your bags; "Gracias, estoy bien" politely declines. Use official airport taxis or a ride app.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Greet your host warmly and ask about the neighborhood. Accepting a coffee or drink when offered is polite.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start with "Disculpe" before your question. Directions often use landmarks like colmados, churches, and well-known businesses.',"Taking the Bus":"Guaguas (minibuses) run set routes; tell the cobrador (fare collector) where you want to get off. In Santo Domingo, OMSA runs city buses and the metro has two lines. Motoconchos (motorcycle taxis) cover short trips.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacies are often the first stop for minor health problems, and pharmacists give direct advice. Many also deliver.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise any issue calmly and quietly with the waiter, and ask for the manager (el encargado) if needed.","Finding an Apartment":"Connections help. Check the water, hot water, and electricity (ask whether there's an inverter or generator for power cuts). Get all terms in writing.","Job Interview":"Titles like Licenciado/a or Ingeniero/a are commonly used. A brief personal exchange before getting to business is standard.","Renting a Car":"Check the insurance and photograph any damage. Watch for motorcycles in traffic, and use GPS outside the main cities.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Having a local contact or abogado (lawyer) to advise you helps, especially if the arrangement was informal.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be warm, specific, and show your documentation. Escalate to the gerente if the front desk doesn't resolve it.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Dominican cooking centers on La Bandera, sancocho, mangú and tostones. Ask about the dish's origins and how the cook learned it.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Baseball, politics, and the economy are common topics. Respectful disagreement is fine.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship before talking numbers is common. A direct counter-offer, made politely, is normal.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A personal, face-to-face apology works best. Explain what you misunderstood.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 911, the national emergency number. Describe the location using nearby landmarks and stay on the line.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday in the DR belongs to family. Sancocho dominicano or La Bandera is likely. Bring something (rum, drinks, a dessert) and plan to stay all afternoon.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Call your rental company or insurance assistance line. On main highways, the Ministry of Public Works (MOPC) runs a free roadside assistance service.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Many people use private clinics for non-emergency care. Bring insurance documents and describe your symptoms specifically: where, since when, and how bad.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Hold your position with reasons and stay respectful. Baseball and politics are the topics most likely to get heated.","Live Radio Call-In":"Radio is a popular source of news and debate. Be direct and specific.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Respect for elders is traditionally important. Choose the right time and a private setting.","Teaching Something":"Use everyday examples and ask what the other person already knows.","At the Bank":"Bring your cédula (ID) or passport. Queues can be long, so arrive early.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":`Most taxis don't use meters, so agree on the fare before you get in: "¿Cuánto cuesta hasta...?" Ride apps like Uber operate in Santo Domingo and Santiago.`,"Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Santo Domingo's Ciudad Colonial is a UNESCO World Heritage Site, home to the Museo de las Casas Reales and the Panteón Nacional. Ask about guided tours."},"Puerto Rico":{"Your First Coffee Order":"Coffee is grown in Puerto Rico's central mountains, around towns like Yauco and Jayuya. A cortadito or café con leche at a cafetería is a classic order.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Chatting with neighbors on the balcón or at the front gate is common, especially in the evening.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Medalla Light is the classic local beer, and Don Q and Bacardí are both made on the island. A piña colada, which San Juan claims as its invention, is a famous local drink.","Throwing a Party":"Food is central: pernil (roast pork), arroz con gandules, and tostones. Guests usually bring something. Salsa and reggaeton are common.","Ordering at a Restaurant":`Puerto Rican food blends Taíno, Spanish, and African influences. Mofongo, lechón, and pasteles are classics. Ask "¿Qué hay hoy?" (What's on today?) for the daily special.`,"Asking for Directions":'Directions often use landmarks rather than street names. "A la vuelta" means around the corner.',"At the Market":"La Placita (Plaza del Mercado) in Santurce sells fresh produce and frituras (fried snacks) by day and becomes a busy nightlife spot on weekends.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":'Guavate, in Cayey, is famous for its lechoneras along "La Ruta del Lechón"; a weekend trip there is a popular outing. Sunday family lunches often run long.',"Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Arroz con gandules and pernil are the classic pairing, especially at holidays. Flan or tembleque (coconut pudding) is a classic dessert.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Interviews often mix US-style structure with some personal small talk. Dress formally, arrive on time, and be ready for behavioral questions.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often start with some personal conversation. Switching between English and Spanish is normal in professional settings.","At the Doctor":"Puerto Rico uses the US healthcare system — insurance, co-pays, and referrals. Many doctors speak both English and Spanish fluently. Walk-in clinics (urgent care) are common for minor issues.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Government offices follow a structure similar to the US mainland. Bring every document required, plus copies, and expect queues.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bring something (a dessert, flowers), greet with a kiss on the cheek if they offer one, and compliment the food. Being "bien educado" (well-mannered) is noticed.',"Negotiating a Price":"At flea markets and some small businesses, friendly negotiation is acceptable. In regular stores, the price is fixed.","Arriving at the Airport":"Luis Muñoz Marín International Airport (SJU) is in Carolina, next to San Juan. Flights from the US mainland are domestic, so there's no passport control. Flying back to the mainland, bags go through a USDA agricultural inspection.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":'The colmado is the neighborhood corner store, a place to grab a cold drink and catch up on local news. Greet with "Buenas" when you walk in.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Greet your host warmly and ask for local tips, like the best nearby beach or lechonera.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start with "Disculpe" before your question. Directions often use local landmarks.',"Taking the Bus":"The Tren Urbano metro runs in San Juan. AMA buses cover the metro area. For most of the island, a car is necessary. Públicos (shared taxis) run between towns.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are normal. Give your name, party size and time clearly; a short friendly exchange first is common.","At the Bank":"US banking rules apply. Banco Popular, FirstBank, and Oriental are major local banks. Bring ID.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Use Uber or Lyft in San Juan. Tourist transport from the airport has fixed rates.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"US pharmacy rules apply -- prescription medications require a prescription. For minor issues, asking the pharmacist for OTC recommendations is completely normal.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"El Morro and Castillo San Cristóbal in Old San Juan are part of the San Juan National Historic Site, run by the US National Park Service. La Fortaleza, the governor's residence, is part of a UNESCO World Heritage Site with them.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call to cancel rather than text. A warm phone call with an apology is all that's needed.","Restaurant Complaint":"US tipping culture applies in Puerto Rico. Raising a complaint is completely acceptable. Be direct but warm.","Finding an Apartment":"Landlords usually ask for income verification and references. Facebook Marketplace and Clasificados Online are common places to look.","Job Interview":"Interviews may be in English, Spanish or both. Dress formally and arrive on time.","Renting a Car":"A US driver's license is valid. Most toll roads are electronic (AutoExpreso), so ask the rental company how tolls are billed. Watch for potholes, and use GPS.","Getting a Haircut":"Barberías are social places. Baseball and basketball are common topics, and so is reggaeton.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Keep all communication in writing. Puerto Rico's Department of Consumer Affairs (DACO) handles many housing and consumer complaints.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"US consumer culture norms apply -- complaining about a genuine billing error is completely normal. Be direct and specific.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Puerto Rican cuisine centers on mofongo, pernil, arroz con gandules, and tostones. Ask about the Taíno, Spanish, and African roots of each dish -- it's a story of three heritages.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"The island's political status (statehood, commonwealth, or independence) is a frequent topic and has been put to several referendums. Baseball and local politics also come up often.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship before getting to the numbers is common. Be clear about what you want.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":`Ask "¿Y tú, pa' dónde vas?" to get the local answer, especially for lechoneras and frituras (fried snacks).`,"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A personal, face-to-face apology works best. Explain what you misunderstood.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 911 -- US emergency services apply in Puerto Rico. Describe your location precisely including the municipality and nearest landmark.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday family lunches in PR are major social events -- pernil, rice and beans, and tostones. Bring something and plan to stay all afternoon with music in the background.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights and call your rental company's roadside assistance or your insurance. Call 911 in an emergency.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"US healthcare rules apply -- bring insurance documentation. Both English and Spanish are used in medical settings across the island.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is common. Island status, hurricane recovery, and the economy are the most charged topics.","Live Radio Call-In":"Radio is a popular platform for political debate. Baseball is another common topic; Roberto Clemente, from Carolina, is one of the island's most celebrated athletes.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Family bonds are traditionally close. Choose the right moment and a private setting.","Teaching Something":"Use everyday examples from island life, and ask what the other person already knows."},Mexico:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Traditional café de olla is brewed with cinnamon and piloncillo (unrefined cane sugar), very different from espresso. Specialty coffee shops are also common in Mexico City.","Ordering at a Restaurant":'"¿Qué nos recomienda?" (What do you recommend?) is a natural opener. Tortillas often come with the meal at no extra charge.',"Negotiating a Price":"At tianguis (street markets) and crafts markets, bargaining (regatear) is normal. At regular stores, prices are fixed.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"The corner tiendita sells everything from tortillas to phone credit. Greet the owner when you walk in.","Job Interview at a Local Company":'A firm handshake, eye contact and questions about the company show interest. Arrive on time and use "usted" until invited otherwise.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"After-work plans might mean tacos, micheladas, or mezcal. Joining for one round is a friendly gesture.","Throwing a Party":"Food is the centerpiece of most celebrations. Piñatas at birthdays are still common, and pozole and tamales are classic party food.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday family lunch is a common tradition, often with dishes like pozole, tamales, or caldo. Plan for a few hours and compliment the cook.","Asking for Directions":'Landmarks ("next to the Oxxo," "past the church") are often more useful than street names. Start with "Disculpe."',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Small talk over the fence or on the street is normal. Asking about family is a friendly topic.","At the Doctor":"Public healthcare (IMSS, ISSSTE) can have long waits; many people use private clinics. Many pharmacies have a doctor's office (consultorio) next door for quick consultations.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring the right paperwork, plus copies. Offices like the SAT (tax authority) often require online appointments, and multiple visits are common.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":'Comida (the midday meal, 2-4pm) is actually the main meal in Mexico, not dinner. Going out to eat at night is lighter. Asking for a doggy bag ("para llevar") is perfectly normal.',"Business Meeting":"Business meetings often open with small talk about family, sports or the city. Expect relationship-building before serious negotiation.","At the Market":"Mercados sell fresh produce, herbs, prepared food and household goods in one place. Vendors often offer a taste (una probadita).","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bring something (dessert, flowers), use "usted" with the parents, and ask about the family. Complimenting the food is appreciated.',"Restaurant Complaint":"Raise the problem directly but politely. Asking for the encargado (manager) is fine if the waiter can't resolve it.","Arriving at the Airport":"At customs, you press a button: green means you can go, and red means your bags will be inspected. Have your accommodation address ready.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Greet your host warmly and ask about the neighborhood. Accepting a drink when offered is polite.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Open with "Disculpe" or "¿Me puede ayudar?" before your question.',"Taking the Bus":`Mexico City's metro has a low flat fare. The Metrobús and metro use a rechargeable card. On peseros (minibuses), have small bills or coins and say "Bajan" or "Aquí, por favor" to get off.`,"Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are normal. Be warm and efficient -- give name, party size, and time clearly. Confirming the day of is common practice in Mexico.","At the Bank":"Many banks use a numbered ticket system. Bring your ID and have your account details ready.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Always agree on the fare BEFORE getting in, or insist on the taxímetro (meter). Uber is widely used and avoids negotiation entirely.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacists can advise on minor ailments, and many pharmacies have an attached doctor's office. Antibiotics require a prescription in Mexico.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"At INAH museums and archaeological sites, Mexican citizens and residents enter free on Sundays. Ask about discounts for students, teachers, and seniors.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call rather than message for important cancellations, and apologize briefly. Many places are flexible with reasonable notice.","Finding an Apartment":"Landlords often require an aval (a guarantor who owns property) or a póliza jurídica (rental guarantee policy), plus a deposit, usually one month's rent.","Job Interview":'Use professional titles like "licenciado/a" or "ingeniero/a" where they apply. A little small talk before business is common.',"Renting a Car":"Mexican liability insurance is required by law, and most US or Canadian policies don't cover driving in Mexico. Watch for topes (speed bumps), even on highways.","Getting a Haircut":"Barbershops and salons are social places. Describe what you want clearly; bringing a photo helps.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document discrepancies immediately and in writing. PROFECO (the consumer protection agency) and a local lawyer can advise you.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite and specific, and show your reservation confirmation. Ask for the gerente (manager) if needed. PROFECO handles consumer complaints.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Traditional Mexican cuisine is on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list. It varies by region: Oaxaca is famous for its moles, Yucatán for cochinita pibil. Ask about the dish's origins.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Politics, football and social issues are all common topics. Specific, informed points work better than broad generalizations.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a personal connection before getting to numbers is common. Be patient and friendly.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "¿Y tú a dónde vas los fines de semana?" (where do you go on weekends?). Asking about comida regional (regional food) opens great conversations.',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A personal, face-to-face apology works best. Explain what you misunderstood.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 911, Mexico's national emergency number since 2017. Describe the location, how many people are hurt, and what happened.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday comida is a traditional family gathering. Arrive close to the invited time and bring something, like beer, a dessert, or flowers for the host.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"The Ángeles Verdes (Green Angels) patrol major highways and provide free roadside assistance -- call 078. On other roads, call your roadside assistance insurance.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Private doctors' offices (consultorios) are widely available. Describe symptoms specifically: where, since when, and how bad.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":`Hold your position firmly but politely, and avoid personal remarks. Using "usted" with people you don't know well keeps things respectful.`,"Live Radio Call-In":"Get to your point quickly and be specific.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Respect for elders is traditionally important. Choose the right time and a private setting.","Teaching Something":"Use practical, real-world examples, and ask what the other person already knows."},Portugal:{"Your First Coffee Order":'In Lisbon, "um café" is called a bica; in Porto, a cimbalino. Both are a short espresso, usually drunk quickly at the counter. A galão (milky coffee in a glass) is a popular breakfast drink.',"Ordering at a Restaurant":"The couvert (bread, olives, butter, cheese) often arrives automatically, but it isn't free. You only pay for what you eat, and you can send it back.","At the Doctor":'Portugal has a national health service (SNS), and many people also use private clinics. Bring your Cartão de Utente (health user card). Doctors are addressed as "Senhor Doutor/Senhora Doutora."',"Business Meeting":`Business settings in Portugal often use formal address ("o senhor" / "a senhora", or a title such as "Doutor"). Arrive on time, and let them know if you'll be late.`,"Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Greet neighbors with "Bom dia" or "Boa tarde." In older neighborhoods, chatting on the street or at the café is common.',"Asking for Directions":'Start with "Com licença" or "Desculpe" (excuse me). In Lisbon, the hills mean directions often include stairs and shortcuts.',"Negotiating a Price":'Negotiation is not common in shops. At markets and antique fairs, a gentle "faz algum desconto?" (any discount?) is acceptable. Being too aggressive is off-putting.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"After-work socializing often means a glass of wine or a cerveja at a nearby tasca (tavern). It's relatively low-key — quality over quantity.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Portions are often generous, and many restaurants offer a meia dose (half portion). The house wine is usually affordable. Dinner is commonly around 8pm.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring every document plus copies. The Finanças (tax office), Junta de Freguesia (parish council), and Loja do Cidadão (citizen's shop) each handle different services. You'll need a NIF (tax number).","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Mention something specific you want to see or eat, and ask for their recommendation. It's an easy way into a real conversation.","At the Market":"The Mercado da Ribeira in Lisbon or Mercado do Bolhão in Porto are institutions. Fresh fish, vegetables, and flowers. Vendors are friendly but businesslike.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Bringing wine, flowers or pastries is a common gesture. Use formal address ("o senhor" / "a senhora") with parents until they invite you to be less formal.',"Arriving at the Airport":"Lisbon's Humberto Delgado Airport connects to the city by metro (red line). Taxis are metered, and ride apps are widely used.","At the Bank":'Bring your NIF (tax number) and passport. Address staff as "senhor/senhora." MB WAY, a Portuguese mobile payment app, is widely used.',"Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Taxis in Lisbon and Porto are metered and regulated, so haggling isn't normal; small extras like luggage can be added legally. Uber and Bolt are widely used alternatives.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Portuguese pharmacists can advise on minor ailments and recommend over-the-counter treatment. Ask for medicamentos genéricos (generics) for lower prices.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The National Tile Museum (Museu Nacional do Azulejo) and the Jerónimos Monastery, a UNESCO World Heritage Site, are Lisbon highlights. Ask about discounts and free-entry days, and bring ID.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel as early as you can, by phone or through the booking platform, and apologize briefly.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues quietly and specifically with the waiter, and ask for the gerente (manager) if needed. Every business in Portugal must have a complaints book (Livro de Reclamações).","Finding an Apartment":"The Lisbon and Porto rental markets are extremely competitive. Prepare a complete dossier. Idealista and OLX are the main platforms. Act quickly when you find something good.","Job Interview":"Portuguese professional culture is formal and somewhat hierarchical. Use 'doutor/doutora' or 'senhor/senhora engenheiro/a' where applicable. Show warmth alongside professional competence.","Renting a Car":"Cars are essential for exploring Portugal outside cities. Toll systems (Via Verde or credit card on electronic tolls) are widespread.","Getting a Haircut":"Portuguese barbershops are professional and unhurried. Football, local news, and the weather are standard conversation. Tipping is appreciated but not mandatory.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Show interest in the company and in growing there. Be warm and professional, and use formal address until invited otherwise.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document discrepancies immediately in writing. The IHRU (housing authority) and DECO (consumer rights) can advise on your rights as a tenant.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be calm, specific, and show your reservation confirmation. Ask to speak with the gerente for any unresolved issues.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Portuguese cuisine has strong regional identity. Ask where the cook is from and what defines their regional cooking.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Specific knowledge and a measured argument carry weight. Respectful disagreement is fine.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building a relationship matters. Be patient, and confirm the final terms in writing.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":"Ask 'Onde é que tu costumas ir?' (where do you usually go?) to get a personal recommendation.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A sincere, personal, face-to-face apology is most effective. Name what went wrong and move on.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112 for all emergencies in Portugal. Give your location first, then what happened.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch (almoço) is a traditional family gathering, often with several courses. Bring wine or pastéis de nata.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle; these are required by law. Call the assistance number on your rental or insurance papers, or 112 in an emergency.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"The national health service (SNS) provides coverage; bring your Cartão de Utente if you have one. Many people also use private clinics for non-emergency care.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Stay composed and back your points with reasons. Historical references, like the Estado Novo dictatorship (1933–1974) and the Carnation Revolution of April 25, 1974, often come up.","Live Radio Call-In":"Rádio Renascença, TSF, and Antena 1 are the main stations. Be direct and specific. Football and politics are the dominant topics.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive topics are best raised face to face, in private. Be honest and kind, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Use practical, everyday examples and check understanding with 'Percebeste?' or 'Faz sentido?'.","Throwing a Party":"Gatherings are usually generous and unhurried; bring something to share. If someone sings fado (on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list), it's traditional to listen in silence.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Greet the shopkeeper with 'bom dia' (or 'boa tarde' after lunch) before anything else, and say 'obrigado' / 'obrigada' on the way out.","Stopping Someone for Help":"Start with 'Com licença' or 'Desculpe'. In hilly cities like Lisbon and Porto, directions often use 'lá em cima' (up there) and 'lá em baixo' (down there), and may include stairs.","Taking the Bus":'In Lisbon, the Viva Viagem or Navegante card works on buses, metro, and trams; in Porto, use the Andante card. Tap or validate when you board. Ask "Este autocarro para em...?" (Does this bus stop at...?)',"Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are standard in Portugal. Give your name, party size, and preferred time. Confirm the date explicitly to avoid any ambiguity."},Castilian:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Café con leche (espresso with hot milk) is the classic breakfast coffee. A cortado is espresso with a splash of milk. Many locals drink at the bar counter.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"Mealtimes are late: lunch around 2–3pm and dinner around 9–10pm. In some cities, especially Granada and León, a free tapa traditionally comes with each drink.","Asking for Directions":'Start with "Perdona" or "Disculpe" before asking. Directions often reference landmarks like bars, plazas, and churches.',"Negotiating a Price":"Bargaining is only acceptable at rastros (flea markets) and second-hand stalls — never in shops or restaurants. At the Rastro in Madrid, light negotiation is expected and part of the fun.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":"Two kisses on the cheek (dos besos) are a common greeting, even when meeting someone new. Chatting in the stairwell or at the local bar is normal.","At the Market":`The mercado sells fresh produce, cured meats and fish at separate stalls. Greet the vendor and ask who's last in line ("¿Quién es el último?") before ordering.`,"Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Dinner in Spain rarely starts before 9pm — 10pm is normal, especially on weekends. Meals are unhurried and social. Nobody expects the bill until you ask for it. Splitting evenly (a la romana) is common among friends.","Job Interview at a Local Company":'Dress well, arrive on time, and show interest in the company. Use "usted" with senior interviewers until invited to switch.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Going for tapas after work is a well-loved tradition: a caña (small draught beer) or a glass of wine with small plates, sometimes moving from bar to bar.","Business Meeting":'Meetings often start with some small talk. Use "usted" with senior people until invited to switch.',"Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Most government offices require an appointment (cita previa), usually booked online. Bring more documents and copies than you think you need.","At the Doctor":"Spain's public health system covers residents; bring your tarjeta sanitaria (health card). Pharmacists can advise on minor ailments, but antibiotics require a prescription.","Throwing a Party":"Parties often start late and go late. Guests commonly bring wine, beer, or something to share.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"The Sunday family lunch is a long-standing tradition, often long and multi-course. Bring wine or dessert. The sobremesa (conversation at the table after the meal) can go on for hours.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":"Two kisses on the cheek, even on first meeting. Bring something (wine, chocolates). Be warm and interested in the family. Compliment the food. Addressing parents informally after the first meeting is fine if they invite it.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise the issue calmly, and ask for the encargado (manager) if needed. Every business in Spain must provide an official complaint form (hoja de reclamaciones) on request.","Breaking Up in a Café":"A café is a public place. Choose a quiet table and keep your voice low if you want privacy.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":'Small neighborhood shops often stay open later than supermarkets. A friendly "Buenas" when you walk in goes a long way.',"Arriving at the Airport":'Customs follows standard EU procedure. Greet officials with "Buenos días" or "Buenas tardes" and answer concisely.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Greet your host and ask for neighborhood tips, like the best local bar or market.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Open with "Perdona" or "Disculpe." Directions often use bars, plazas, and churches as landmarks.',"Taking the Bus":"In Madrid, buses (EMT) and the metro use the rechargeable Tarjeta Transporte Público; in Barcelona, use a T-casual or similar card. The metro is usually the fastest option in both cities.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are normal at midrange and upscale restaurants. State name, party size, date, and time directly. Confirming the same day is common practice.","At the Bank":"Bring your NIE, DNI or passport. Many branches use numbered tickets, so take one when you arrive.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Spanish taxis are metered and regulated, so no haggling. Madrid has an official flat fare between the airport and the city center. Ask for a receipt (recibo).","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Spanish pharmacists (look for the green cross) can advise on minor ailments and recommend over-the-counter treatment. Antibiotics require a prescription.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The Prado and the Reina Sofía offer free entry during set hours, usually the last couple of hours of the day; check current times online. Ask about reduced prices (entrada reducida) for students and seniors.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call to cancel rather than message, apologize briefly and explain. Ask whether there is any fee.","Finding an Apartment":"Rentals in Madrid and Barcelona are competitive. Prepare payslips, your NIE, and your employment contract. By law, the deposit (fianza) for a housing rental is one month's rent.","Job Interview":'Use "usted" until invited to switch. Show enthusiasm for the role alongside your credentials.',"Renting a Car":"Check the car carefully and document any damage. Some motorways (autopistas de peaje) charge tolls, while autovías are generally free. Speed cameras are common.","Getting a Haircut":"Describe the cut you want clearly; bringing a photo helps. Football and weekend plans are common small talk.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Spanish tenancy law (the Ley de Arrendamientos Urbanos, LAU) sets out tenants' rights. Document discrepancies immediately in writing.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":'Be calm and specific, with your booking confirmation in hand. "Quisiera hablar con el director/la directora" is your escalation, and you can ask for the hoja de reclamaciones (official complaint form).',"Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Spanish cuisine is strongly regional: Basque, Catalan, Andalusian, and Galician cooking are all distinct. Ask where the cook is from and what defines their regional cuisine.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Politics, football, and regional identity are common topics. A clear, specific position is respected; keep it about the ideas.","Negotiating a Deal":`State your position clearly and leave room for compromise. "Venga, nos ponemos de acuerdo" (come on, let's reach an agreement) signals willingness to deal.`,"Getting a Local's Recommendation":'"¿Y tú, a dónde irías?" (Where would you go?) gets past the tourist answer. Asking about regional food opens great conversations.',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":'A clear explanation and a simple apology ("perdona, no lo sabía") usually work best.',"Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112 for all emergencies in Spain. State your location precisely using the road number and nearest kilometre marker.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch can last for hours. Arrive with wine, dessert, or flowers. The sobremesa, talking at the table after the meal, is a beloved tradition.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights and put on the reflective vest. Since 2026, Spain requires a connected V-16 emergency light on the car's roof instead of warning triangles. Then call your insurance or rental assistance line, or 112.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Your médico de cabecera (GP) at the centro de salud handles most things. Bring your tarjeta sanitaria.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Hold your ground with specific facts and stay respectful.","Live Radio Call-In":"Cadena SER, COPE, and Onda Cero are major talk-radio stations. Be specific and get to your point quickly.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive conversations are often best held face to face, sometimes during or after a meal. Be honest and kind, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Explain the why behind things, and invite questions throughout."},Colombia:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Colombia is one of the world's largest coffee producers, and its Coffee Cultural Landscape is a UNESCO World Heritage Site. A tinto (small black coffee) is offered constantly, in offices, homes, and from street vendors.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"Bandeja paisa, from the Antioquia region, piles rice, beans, chicharrón, chorizo, egg, arepa, and plantain on one plate. Fresh juices (mora, lulo, maracuyá) are common with meals.","Asking for Directions":'In Bogotá, addresses follow a grid: Calles run east–west and Carreras north–south, and an address tells you the nearest cross street. Start with "Disculpe."',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":'In Bogotá and Antioquia, many people use "usted" even with friends and family; it signals respect, not distance.',"At the Market":"Paloquemao in Bogotá is a famous market, full of tropical fruits, vegetables, and flowers. Ask to try unfamiliar fruits like lulo, granadilla, or guanábana.","Job Interview at a Local Company":'Titles are commonly used: professionals are often addressed as "Doctor/Doctora" even without a doctorate, as a sign of respect. Arrive on time.',"After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Aguardiente, an anise-flavored liquor, is a classic Colombian drink, and Club Colombia is a popular beer. Joining for one round is a friendly gesture.","Throwing a Party":"Big celebrations often feature lechona (stuffed roast pig) and arepas. Cumbia and vallenato, which is on UNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage list, are classic party music.","Business Meeting":"Meetings often open with small talk. Use titles like Doctor or Doctora for professionals until invited otherwise.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday ajiaco (chicken and potato soup, especially in Bogotá) or a full bandeja is a common Sunday meal. Bringing a dessert is appreciated.","At the Doctor":'Most people are covered through an EPS (health insurer); private clinics are also common. Doctors are addressed as "Doctor/Doctora."',"Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Bring the specific documents required, plus copies. Key offices include the DIAN (tax authority) and the Registraduría (civil registry and IDs).","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Soup is a common first course, especially in the menú del día (set lunch). A tinto (black coffee) often ends the meal.","Negotiating a Price":"At informal markets, light bargaining is acceptable. At established businesses, prices are fixed. Courtesy goes a long way.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Arrive on time, bring something (flowers, sweets, wine), use "usted" with the parents, and ask about the family. Being "bien educado" (well-mannered) is noticed.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Ask your host for local tips, like a neighborhood bakery, the best empanada stand, or a nearby park.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"The tienda de barrio is the neighborhood corner store, and many owners extend credit to regulars. A greeting before you order is expected.","Arriving at the Airport":"Bogotá's El Dorado is one of the busiest airports in Latin America. Use the official taxi line or a ride app rather than accepting rides from people approaching you.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Open with "Disculpe" or "Con permiso" before your question.',"Taking the Bus":`Bogotá's TransMilenio uses the rechargeable TuLlave card. Medellín has the only metro system in Colombia. On regular buses, press the bell or tell the driver "Me deja aquí, por favor."`,"Calling to Make a Reservation":'"Con mucho gusto" (with pleasure) is a very common courteous phrase. Give your name, party size, and time clearly.',"At the Bank":"Bring your ID and the full set of documents required. Queues can be long, so arrive early.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Ride apps or taxis called through an app or official line are the safest options. For street taxis, check that the meter is running, or agree on the fare before you leave.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Droguerías (pharmacies) are everywhere, and pharmacists can advise on minor ailments. Antibiotics require a prescription.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Bogotá's Gold Museum (Museo del Oro) is free on Sundays, and the Botero Museum is free every day. Ask about discounts for students and seniors.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Cancel as early as you can with a brief apology and explanation.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues quietly and privately with the waiter, and ask for the encargado (manager) if needed.","Finding an Apartment":"Have full documentation ready -- income proof, ID, and a codeudor (guarantor) is often required. Read the contrato carefully.","Job Interview":'Colombian professional culture is formal. Addressing professionals as "Doctor/Doctora" is common. Use "usted" until invited otherwise.',"Renting a Car":"Check the insurance and photograph any damage. Bogotá and Medellín restrict driving on certain days based on license plate numbers (pico y placa), so check before you drive.","Getting a Haircut":"Barbershops are social places. Football, especially local teams, and neighborhood news are common topics.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document discrepancies immediately and get everything in writing. A local lawyer can advise you on your rights as a tenant.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Be polite and specific, and show your documentation. Ask to speak with the gerente for unresolved issues.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Colombian cuisine varies by region: the Caribbean coast, the Andean highlands, the Pacific, and the Llanos all have distinct dishes. Ask where the cook is from.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"The armed conflict and the 2016 peace agreement shape many political conversations. Listen carefully and show specific knowledge.","Negotiating a Deal":'Building a relationship comes first. Stay courteous throughout; "con mucho gusto" goes a long way.',"Getting a Local's Recommendation":`Ask "¿Y usted adónde suele ir?" (where do you usually go?) for a personal recommendation, and what's typical from their home region.`,"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A personal, direct apology works best. Explain what you misunderstood.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 123 (Colombia's unified emergency number). Describe your location clearly -- include the locality and nearest visible landmark.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":'Sunday lunch is a traditional family gathering. Use "usted" with elders and accept food when offered. If people start dancing, joining in is welcome but not required.',"Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights and place your warning signals. Call your insurance or rental assistance line; on highways, dial #767 from a mobile phone to reach the highway police, or 123 for emergencies.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Bring your EPS (health insurer) card and any previous records. Describe symptoms specifically: where, since when, and how bad.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is fine; personal insults are not. Specific knowledge and clear reasoning earn respect.","Live Radio Call-In":"Caracol Radio, RCN Radio, and Blu Radio are major national stations. Be specific and get to your point quickly.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Respect for elders is traditionally important. Sensitive conversations are best held in person, at the right time and in private.","Teaching Something":"Invite questions and use practical, everyday examples."},"Northern Italian":{"Your First Coffee Order":'Coffee is usually drunk standing at the bar ("al banco"); table service often costs more. A cappuccino is a morning drink. In the north, try a "marocchino": espresso with cocoa and milk foam.',"Ordering at a Restaurant":'Classic Milanese dishes are risotto alla milanese (with saffron), cotoletta alla milanese (a breaded veal cutlet) and ossobuco. A "coperto" (cover charge) per person is common on the bill.',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Many Milan apartment buildings have a portinaio or portinaia (concierge), who knows everyone in the building. A polite "buongiorno" in the stairwell goes a long way.',"Asking for Directions":'Start with "Mi scusi." The Duomo and metro stations are the usual landmarks. Metro lines are known by number and color: M1 red, M2 green, M3 yellow, M4 blue, M5 lilac.',"At the Market":"Milan has open-air neighborhood markets (mercati rionali) on set days of the week, such as the one on Viale Papiniano. Greet the stallholder before asking for anything.","Negotiating a Price":"Prices in shops are fixed. At flea markets, like the Saturday Fiera di Senigallia by the Darsena, asking for a small discount is normal.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch is a family occasion. Bring pastries from a pasticceria or a bottle of wine, and compliment the cooking.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"The aperitivo, from about 6 to 9 pm, is Milan's after-work ritual: a drink that comes with snacks, sometimes a full buffet. Football (Inter or Milan) is a popular topic.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":'Try a risotto alla milanese or a cotoletta. Dinner usually starts around 8 pm, and the bill comes only when you ask for it ("il conto, per favore").',"Throwing a Party":"Guests usually bring a bottle of wine or a dessert. In December, a panettone is a classic gift.","Business Meeting":`Milan is Italy's main financial and business center, home to the Borsa Italiana. Be on time, use "Lei", and use professional titles like "Dottore", "Ingegnere" or "Avvocato" until invited otherwise.`,"Meeting the Parents for the First Time":'Use "Lei" with the parents until they suggest "tu". Bring pastries or flowers, but avoid chrysanthemums, which in Italy are associated with funerals.',"Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Many services from the Comune di Milano are booked online. Bring every document plus copies, and your codice fiscale (tax code). Patience and politeness help.","Arriving at the Airport":"Linate is close to the city and connected by the M4 metro line. Malpensa is farther out; the Malpensa Express train runs to Cadorna and Centrale stations. Taxis from Malpensa to central Milan have a fixed fare.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":'Greet the shopkeeper with "Buongiorno" or "Buonasera" when you walk in, and say "arrivederci" when you leave.',"Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"By Italian law, hosts must register guests' ID documents with the police, so expect to show your passport. Asking for a favorite local aperitivo spot is a great conversation starter.","At the Doctor":"In Lombardy, public health care is organized through the ATS and ASST system. Bring your tessera sanitaria (health card) if you have one, and describe your symptoms in standard Italian.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Always start with "Scusi." People in central Milan are often in a hurry, so keep your question short and clear.',"Taking the Bus":"ATM runs Milan's metro, trams and buses. One ticket covers about 90 minutes of travel in the city, and you can tap a contactless card at metro turnstiles. Some historic 1920s trams are still in service.","Calling to Make a Reservation":'Popular restaurants fill up, especially on weekends and during Design Week. Give your name, the time and the number of people; "grazie, a stasera" closes the call.',"At the Bank":"Bring an ID document and your codice fiscale. Many routine operations are faster in the bank's app.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Official taxis are white and use a meter, so there is no haggling. Taxis are usually taken at a rank or booked by phone or app rather than hailed in the street. From Malpensa, confirm the fixed fare before you set off.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":'Pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments. A "farmacia di turno" stays open at night.',"Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The Pinacoteca di Brera and the Castello Sforzesco museums are highlights. For Leonardo's Last Supper, visits are in small timed groups, so book well ahead.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call or use the booking app as early as you can, and be brief and polite.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise issues politely and privately with the waiter. Framing it as a question works better than an accusation.","Finding an Apartment":`Milan is one of Italy's most expensive rental markets. Common contract types are the "4+4" and the "3+2" (canone concordato); agencies usually charge a fee. Check heating costs and condominium fees.`,"Job Interview":'Be punctual and well prepared. Interviews in Milan are often fairly formal, so use "Lei" unless invited otherwise.',"Renting a Car":"Central Milan has Area C, a charged zone for cars, and a wider low-emission zone (Area B). Blue lines mark paid parking and yellow lines are for residents. Public transport is often easier inside the city.","Getting a Haircut":"Book ahead with the barbiere or parrucchiere. Small talk about the neighborhood or football is normal.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"Punctuality and preparation matter. A referral from a mutual contact can help.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document everything immediately and in writing. A tenants' association or a local lawyer (avvocato) can advise you on your rights.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Milan charges a tourist tax (imposta di soggiorno) per person per night, often paid separately at the hotel. Be specific and calm, and show your booking.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":`Ask about risotto technique: toasting the rice ("tostatura") and adding broth little by little. The cotoletta alla milanese is a breaded veal cutlet, sometimes pounded thin into an "orecchia d'elefante" (elephant ear).`,"Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Common topics are housing costs in Milan, football, and the 2026 Milano Cortina Winter Olympics. Respectful disagreement is fine.","Negotiating a Deal":"Come prepared with numbers. A clear, polite counter-offer is normal.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Tu dove vai di solito?" (where do you usually go?): their favorite aperitivo bar, trattoria or place for a cotoletta.',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A direct, sincere apology works best. Say what happened and move forward.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112, the EU emergency number, which handles all emergencies in Lombardy. Give the location first, then what happened.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Arrive with pastries from a pasticceria or a bottle of wine. In winter, risotto or polenta may be on the table.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle. Call ACI roadside assistance (803 116) or the number on your rental or insurance papers.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms specifically, and bring any prior documents and your tessera sanitaria. Writing your symptoms down in Italian beforehand helps.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Lively debate is a normal part of conversation. Hold your position with specific facts and stay respectful.","Live Radio Call-In":"Radio Popolare, founded in Milan in 1976, is a well-known local station with call-in shows. Local knowledge, like neighborhoods and metro lines, helps you make your point.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Sensitive conversations are best held face to face. Be honest and kind, and listen.","Teaching Something":"Expect direct, practical questions. Show examples and keep it organized."},Sicilian:{"Your First Coffee Order":"A classic Sicilian summer breakfast is granita con brioche: almond, lemon, or coffee granita with a soft brioche bun.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"Sicilian food reflects Greek, Arab, Norman, and Spanish influences. Try arancine (called arancini in eastern Sicily), pasta con le sarde, and caponata. Ask what's local and seasonal.","Chatting With Your Neighbor":'Greet neighbors with "Buongiorno" or "Buonasera." The passeggiata, an evening stroll through town, is a classic time to chat.',"At the Market":"Ballarò, the Vucciria, and Il Capo in Palermo are famous street markets, full of fish, spices, produce, and street food like panelle and arancine.","Asking for Directions":`Many Sicilians speak both Italian and Sicilian. If you don't catch something, ask: "Può ripetere più lentamente?" Churches and piazzas are the usual landmarks.`,"Negotiating a Price":"At markets and antique stalls, asking for a small discount is normal. In regular shops, prices are fixed. Be polite and unhurried.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday lunch is a long family meal, often featuring pasta al forno (baked pasta) or slow-cooked meat, and ending with sweets like cassata or cannoli.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Near the coast, seafood is the focus. Don't skip dessert: cannoli and cassata are Sicilian classics.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"Show curiosity about specifically Sicilian culture, including its food, history, and language, not just Italian culture in general.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"An early-evening aperitivo with local wine, arancine, and olives is a common way to socialize.","Throwing a Party":"Sicilian celebrations are centered on food and family. Seasonal produce, slow-cooked dishes, and homemade sweets. Music is present but background — the table is the main event.","Business Meeting":"Relationships are built over time, and a personal introduction through a mutual connection can help. Use formal titles.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"Expect repeated visits. Keep copies of everything and stay patient and polite.","Meeting the Parents for the First Time":`Family ties are traditionally close. Bring pastries from a local pasticceria, use "Lei" with the parents, and try what you're offered.`,"Arriving at the Airport":"The main airports are Palermo Falcone-Borsellino and Catania Fontanarossa. Both have shuttle buses into the city; Palermo also has a train link. For taxis, confirm the fare before you leave.","Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Greet the shopkeeper first and be warm and brief. The local panetteria or alimentari is a social hub in every Sicilian neighborhood.","Stopping Someone for Help":"Always 'Scusi' first. Directions often reference piazzas and churches. Expect helpful, sometimes elaborate responses.","Taking the Bus":"Interbus and AST are the main intercity options. City buses exist but are infrequent in smaller towns. Buy tickets in advance from tobacconists.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are standard. Sicilian restaurants take hospitality seriously -- a warm, clear booking call sets the right tone.","At the Bank":"Sicilian banks can be slow and paper-heavy. Bring full ID and any relevant documents. Address staff formally and warmly.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Taxis in Sicilian cities are metered. For private transfers, agree on the price upfront.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"Pharmacies (look for the green cross) are staffed by trained pharmacists who can advise on minor ailments and over-the-counter treatment.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"Sicily's archaeological sites include the Valley of the Temples in Agrigento (a UNESCO World Heritage Site), Segesta, and Selinunte. Ask about riduzioni (reduced tickets) and free-entry days.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Call as early as you can and apologize briefly.","Restaurant Complaint":"Raise any issue privately and calmly. Framing it as a question works better than an accusation.","Finding an Apartment":"Personal connections can help. Inspect thoroughly and get the contract in writing before you pay.","Job Interview":"Personal warmth matters alongside credentials. Use formal address throughout.","Renting a Car":"A car makes it much easier to explore the island. Check your insurance carefully. Rural roads can be narrow, and many historic centers are restricted traffic zones (ZTL).","Getting a Haircut":"The barbiere is a neighborhood fixture. Football and local news are common topics.","Job Interview at a Local Company":"A referral from a mutual contact can help. Show interest in the company's local roots.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Document everything immediately and in writing. A local lawyer (avvocato) can advise you on your rights.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Stay calm and specific, with your paperwork in hand. Frame it as a fix you need, not an accusation.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"Sicily was under Arab rule from the 9th to the 11th century, which shaped its food: citrus, almonds, and sugar cane all feature in Sicilian cooking. Ask the cook about this history.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Football, politics, and the island's relationship with the mainland are common topics. Respectful disagreement is fine.","Negotiating a Deal":"Building trust takes time. Be patient, and confirm the final terms in writing.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":'Ask "Dove mangiate voi?" (Where do you all eat?). Asking about local specialties opens great conversations.',"Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"A direct, sincere, face-to-face apology lands best in Sicily. Indirect communication about a genuine issue can seem evasive.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 112, the EU emergency number; 118 also reaches medical emergency services. Give the nearest town name and the road number.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday lunch is a multi-course family meal that can last hours. Bring something from a pasticceria, like cannoli or a cassata.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Turn on your hazard lights, put on the reflective vest, and place the warning triangle. Call ACI roadside assistance (803 116) and give the road number (SS or SP) and the nearest kilometre marker.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"Describe symptoms precisely. Public healthcare is run locally by the ASP (Azienda Sanitaria Provinciale); many people also use private clinics. Bring your tessera sanitaria if you have one.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Hold your position with clear reasons. Sicily's long history, with Greek, Roman, Arab, Norman, and Spanish rule, often comes up in debates.","Live Radio Call-In":"Regional Sicilian radio stations cover local politics and football (especially Palermo and Catania). Showing knowledge of local issues gets you respect immediately.","Difficult Family Conversation":"Choose the setting and timing carefully, and show extra respect when speaking with elders.","Teaching Something":"Showing the practical application, not just the theory, works well. Asking what they already know shows respect and leads to better teaching.","At the Doctor":"Public healthcare (ASP) can have long waits for non-urgent appointments; many people also use private clinics. Describe symptoms specifically and bring your tessera sanitaria."},American:{"Your First Coffee Order":"Customizing your order (milk type, size, extra shots) is completely normal. Tipping at coffee shops is optional; many people leave $1 or pick a tip option on the screen.","Ordering at a Restaurant":"Tipping 15–20% at sit-down restaurants is customary, since servers rely on tips for much of their pay. Taking leftovers home in a to-go box is normal, and splitting the check is common.","Asking for Directions":'In many cities, directions use cross streets and blocks ("two blocks down, then left on Oak"). Most people use GPS, but asking a stranger is perfectly fine.',"Chatting With Your Neighbor":`American neighbors range from very friendly to barely acquainted. A wave and a "hey, how are you?" (not expecting a real answer) is standard. If they bring over baked goods, that's a genuine welcome.`,"At the Doctor":"Bring your insurance card and photo ID; check-in usually starts with these. You may pay a co-pay at the visit, and some insurance plans require a referral to see a specialist.","Job Interview at a Local Company":'American interviews are structured around behavioral questions: "Tell me about a time you..." Research the company thoroughly and have questions ready for the end. Firm handshake, eye contact, genuine enthusiasm.',"Job Interview":'Prepare a short answer to "Tell me about yourself." Negotiating salary is common. A thank-you email within 24 hours is standard practice.',"At the Bank":"American banks are transactional and efficient. Bring photo ID. Tellers will offer to open additional accounts or credit cards — you can politely decline. Customer service is generally friendly and direct.","Negotiating a Price":"Bargaining is not normal at most stores. At flea markets, garage sales, and car dealerships, negotiation is expected. At a dealership, starting below the sticker price and negotiating up is the standard process.","After-Work Drinks With Colleagues":"Happy hour (typically 4-7pm) is the American after-work ritual — discounted drinks at a nearby bar. A round or two is standard. Running a tab is common. Conversation is usually light — sports, weekend plans.","Business Meeting":"American meetings are agenda-driven and time-conscious. Start on time, get to the point, end with clear action items. Small talk happens at the start but briefly. Following up by email after is standard.","Throwing a Party":"House parties are often BYOB (bring your own bottle). The host provides some food, and guests often bring snacks or a dish to share.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's House":"Sunday brunch culture is big — pancakes, eggs, bacon, and bottomless coffee or mimosas. It's social and relaxed. Sunday dinner (roast chicken, BBQ) is more family-oriented and often multigenerational.","Dealing with Bureaucracy":"The DMV (Department of Motor Vehicles) is famous for long waits, so book an appointment online if you can. You need a REAL ID–compliant license or a passport to board domestic flights.","Dinner at a Local Restaurant":"Dinner is commonly between 6 and 8pm. The server often brings the check without being asked; it doesn't mean you have to leave right away.","At the Market":"Farmers markets are often held on weekend mornings. Prices are usually fixed, and vendors are happy to tell you how things were grown.","At the Pharmacy":"Many medicines need a prescription, but there's a large over-the-counter section. Show your insurance card for prescriptions, and ask the pharmacist about OTC options.","Medical Emergency":"Call 911 for emergencies. By federal law, hospital emergency rooms must screen and stabilize anyone, regardless of insurance, but bills can be very high without coverage. Urgent care clinics handle non-life-threatening issues.","Calling to Make a Reservation":"Phone reservations are still common at nicer restaurants. Be ready to give your name, party size, and a phone number. OpenTable and Resy apps have largely replaced phone calls at most places.","Changing or Cancelling a Reservation":"Many restaurants charge fees for late cancellations or no-shows. Cancel as early as you can, through the app (OpenTable, Resy) or by phone.","Getting a Haircut":"American barbershops range from quick and transactional to social neighborhood hubs. Tipping 15-20% is expected. Describing what you want precisely matters — the barber won't guess.","Getting a Local's Recommendation":`Ask "Where do you actually go?" rather than "Where's good?"; it signals you want the real answer.`,"Meeting the Parents for the First Time":"American parent meetings are generally relaxed compared to more formal cultures. Bring a small gift (wine, flowers), use first names if invited to, and show genuine interest. A firm handshake and warmth goes a long way.","Stopping Someone for Help":'Start with "Excuse me" and be specific about what you need: "Excuse me, do you know how to get to…?"',"Buying Something at a Corner Store":"Corner stores (bodegas in NYC, party stores in the Midwest) are quick and transactional. Small talk with the owner is friendly but brief. Self-checkout is increasingly common.","Arriving at the Airport":"On arrival, CBP (Customs and Border Protection) officers check your documents; answer questions clearly and briefly. At TSA security, liquids in carry-ons must be 3.4 oz (100 ml) or less per container.","Meeting Your Airbnb Host":"American Airbnb hosts are generally warm but respect personal space. A brief, friendly introduction is the norm. Leave the space as you found it; it's taken seriously.","Taking the Bus":"Public transit varies by city. New York and Chicago have extensive subway and train systems; many other cities are more car-dependent. Many systems accept contactless card payment.","Haggling With a Taxi Driver":"Taxis are metered, so don't haggle. Uber and Lyft are widely used. Tipping drivers 15–20% is customary.","Describing Symptoms at a Pharmacy":"American pharmacies require a prescription for many medications. The pharmacist can give advice on OTC (over-the-counter) options. Show your insurance card -- costs vary dramatically.","Restaurant Complaint":"Politely sending food back or raising a problem is normal. Be specific and calm.","Finding an Apartment":"American rental applications require credit check, income verification (usually 3x monthly rent), and references. No pets clauses are common. Read the lease carefully.","Renting a Car":"You'll need a valid driver's license, and visitors are often advised to carry an International Driving Permit. Check whether your credit card covers rental insurance before buying it at the desk.","Apartment Hunting Gone Wrong":"Check your lease and your state and local tenant laws. Keep all communication in writing. Online reviews of the landlord or building can flag known issues.","Disputing a Charge at a Hotel":"Hotels respond well to direct complaints. Ask to speak with the manager. Leaving an accurate review afterward is considered a normal part of consumer feedback culture.","Cooking Class With a Local Chef":"American culinary culture has regional depth -- Southern BBQ, Cajun Louisiana, New England seafood, Tex-Mex. Ask where the chef is from and what defines their regional cooking.","Debating a News Story With a Friend":"Depending on where you are, political discussions can be either totally open or carefully avoided. Reading the room matters. Americans generally respect directness paired with specific facts.","Negotiating a Deal":"American negotiation is direct and transactional. State your position clearly, back it with specifics, and be ready to close. 'What can you do on the price?' is a completely normal opener.","Explaining a Cultural Misunderstanding":"Americans respond well to a direct, sincere apology -- 'I'm sorry, I didn't realize, here's what I should have done differently.' Being vague about what went wrong reads as insincere.","Helping at an Accident Scene":"Call 911 immediately. Give your location, what happened, and how many people are hurt, and stay on the line.","Sunday Lunch at a Friend's Family Home":"Sunday family dinners in the US vary enormously by region and family. Bring something -- wine, a pie, flowers -- and ask in advance if there are dietary restrictions.","Your Car Broke Down, Calling for Help":"Call AAA if you're a member -- 1-800-222-4357. On highways, move to the shoulder, turn on hazard lights, and stay in the car until help arrives.","Doctor's Appointment Without a Translator":"American healthcare is expensive without insurance. Urgent care clinics are cheaper than ER visits for non-life-threatening issues. Always ask about cost before any test or procedure.","Arguing Your Corner in a Heated Discussion":"Americans debate directly but usually with the understanding that disagreement isn't personal. Back up your position with specific facts. 'I see your point, and I'd add...' is a productive framing.","Live Radio Call-In":"Call-in radio is politically charged in the US. Get to your point quickly. Being specific, direct, and confident is what keeps you on air.","Difficult Family Conversation":"American family communication varies enormously. Directness paired with emotional awareness works well. 'I need to talk to you about something important' is a recognized signal.","Teaching Something":"American learners respond well to practical, applied instruction. Get to the point quickly. Use examples from everyday American life. Encourage questions throughout rather than at the end.","Buying Tickets at a Museum":"The Smithsonian museums in Washington, DC are free. At the Met in New York, pay-what-you-wish applies only to New York State residents and some local students; others pay a fixed price. Booking online can save time."}};export{e as C};
